const CURATED_CASELAWS = {
  "s3-crimes:u1:6": [
    { name:"K.M. Nanavati v. State of Maharashtra", cite:"AIR 1962 SC 605",
      facts:"Naval Commander K.M. Nanavati shot and killed his wife's lover, Prem Ahuja, after his wife confessed to the affair. He claimed the killing occurred in a sudden fit of passion after confronting Ahuja and demanding to know if he would marry his wife and take responsibility for their children.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held that the gap between the confession and the shooting — Nanavati dropped his family at a cinema, went to his ship for a pistol, then drove to Ahuja's house — showed a 'cooling-off' period, so the act was not committed in the heat of the moment. This is the standard case for testing mens rea against a claimed loss of self-control, and for how courts assess 'grave and sudden provocation'." },
    { name:"State of Maharashtra v. M.H. George", cite:"AIR 1965 SC 722",
      facts:"George, a foreign national in transit through Bombay, was found carrying gold in violation of the Foreign Exchange Regulation Act. He argued he had no knowledge of the specific Indian regulation and so lacked mens rea, believing he was merely in transit and not subject to Indian customs law.",
      holding:"The Court held that for certain regulatory/economic offences, the statute can impose strict liability — the prosecution need not prove mens rea where the legislature's object (protecting the economy) requires strict enforcement. The go-to case for the exception to the general mens rea requirement." },
    { name:"R v. Prince (widely cited in Indian jurisprudence)", cite:"(1875) LR 2 CCR 154",
      facts:"Prince took an unmarried girl under 16 out of her father's possession without consent, honestly and reasonably believing she was 18 based on her appearance and her own statement.",
      holding:"The court held Prince guilty regardless of his honest belief about her age, because the underlying act was itself wrongful — moral involuntariness does not excuse liability where part of the actus reus is a strict-liability element. Used alongside BNS provisions on offences against children to explain why mistake of fact is a narrower defense than most students assume." }
  ],
  "s1-tort:u1:2": [
    { name:"Ashby v. White", cite:"(1703) 92 ER 126; 2 Ld Raym 938",
      facts:"Matthew Ashby, a qualified voter in a parliamentary election at Aylesbury, was wrongfully prevented from casting his vote by White, the local returning officer, who wrongly claimed Ashby was not a settled inhabitant. Ashby's preferred candidate went on to win the election anyway — he suffered no actual financial or material loss.",
      holding:"Chief Justice Holt held that the wrongful denial of a legal right is itself actionable even without any actual damage — 'every injury imports a damage, though it does not cost the party one farthing.' This established the doctrine of injuria sine damno (legal injury without material loss) and the maxim ubi jus ibi remedium (where there is a right, there is a remedy) — foundational for understanding when a tort claim can succeed even absent measurable financial harm, contrasted with damnum sine injuria (loss without a legal injury, not actionable — e.g. the Gloucester Grammar School Case, where a competing school causing financial loss through lawful competition gave no remedy)." }
  ],
  "s1-tort:u1:3": [
    { name:"Padmavati v. Dugganaika", cite:"Karnataka High Court, 1975",
      facts:"The driver of a jeep took it out to fill petrol; two strangers asked for and were given a lift without the owner's knowledge or permission. While travelling, a bolt in the wheel failed — a latent defect not detectable through ordinary maintenance checks — causing the jeep to overturn. One of the gratuitous passengers died from the resulting injuries.",
      holding:"The Karnataka High Court held that neither the driver nor the owner was liable: the accident was a sheer accident caused by a latent, undetectable defect (no negligence), and separately, the passengers had voluntarily and knowingly boarded the jeep without invitation, so the defence of volenti non fit injuria ('to a willing person, no injury is done') applied. The standard Indian case for teaching this defence, usually taught alongside English cases like Hall v. Brooklands Auto Racing Club." }
  ],
  "s1-tort:u2:0": [
    { name:"State of Rajasthan v. Vidyawati", cite:"AIR 1962 SC 933",
      facts:"A government jeep, maintained for the official use of the Collector of Udaipur, was being driven back from a workshop after repairs by a temporary driver, Lokumal, who drove rashly and negligently, killing a pedestrian, Jagdishlal. His widow Vidyawati sued both the driver and the State of Rajasthan for compensation; the State claimed sovereign immunity.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held that maintaining a vehicle and driver for official use is a non-sovereign function — an ordinary employer-type activity — so the State, like any other employer, is vicariously liable for its employee's tortious acts committed within the course of employment. Rejected the blanket claim of sovereign immunity, distinguishing between sovereign functions (immune) and non-sovereign/commercial-type functions (not immune) — a distinction later tested again in Kasturilal (see 'Doctrine of Sovereign Immunity')." },
    { name:"Rylands v. Fletcher", cite:"(1868) LR 3 HL 330 (foundational — basis for Indian strict/absolute liability)",
      facts:"Fletcher had a reservoir built on his land to supply water to his mill. Unknown to him, the contractors failed to properly seal disused mine shafts beneath it, and water escaped into Rylands' neighbouring coal mine, causing extensive damage.",
      holding:"The House of Lords held that a person who brings something onto their land likely to cause harm if it escapes is strictly liable for the damage if it does escape, regardless of negligence. This is the origin of 'strict liability', later expanded by the Indian Supreme Court in M.C. Mehta v. Union of India (1987) into 'absolute liability' with no exceptions, for hazardous industries." }
  ],
  "s1-tort:u2:1": [
    { name:"Donoghue v. Stevenson", cite:"[1932] AC 562 (House of Lords, foundational — widely applied in India)",
      facts:"Mrs Donoghue drank ginger beer from an opaque bottle bought for her by a friend. A decomposed snail was found in the bottle after she had consumed part of it, and she became ill. She could not sue the manufacturer in contract since she was not the buyer.",
      holding:"Lord Atkin established the 'neighbour principle' — a manufacturer owes a duty of care to the final consumer, regardless of privity of contract, if it's reasonably foreseeable that a lack of care could injure them. This is the origin case for the modern law of negligence and is cited in nearly every Indian negligence judgment as the foundation of 'duty of care'." },
    { name:"Municipal Corporation of Delhi v. Subhagwanti", cite:"AIR 1966 SC 1750",
      facts:"A clock tower in Chandni Chowk, Delhi, which was over 80 years old and in a dilapidated condition, collapsed and killed several people. The Municipal Corporation, responsible for its maintenance, claimed it had no specific knowledge the structure was about to fall.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court applied the doctrine of res ipsa loquitur ('the thing speaks for itself') — since a properly maintained structure does not collapse without negligence, the burden shifted to the Corporation to prove it was not negligent, which it failed to do. Key Indian case for res ipsa loquitur and municipal/occupier liability." }
  ],
  "s1-legal:u2:1": [
    { name:"Heydon's Case (foundational — mischief rule, applied throughout Indian statutory interpretation)", cite:"(1584) 76 ER 637; [1584] EWHC Exch J36",
      facts:"Ottery College had granted a tenancy in its manor to Ware (father and son) for their lives. Parliament then passed the Suppression of Religious Houses Act, 1535, dissolving religious colleges but protecting grants made more than a year before the Act. The college separately leased the same land to Heydon for 80 years, and a dispute arose over whether Heydon's later lease survived the dissolution.",
      holding:"The Court of Exchequer held Heydon's lease void, and in doing so laid down four questions courts must ask when interpreting an ambiguous statute: what was the law before the Act, what mischief/defect did the Act address, what remedy did Parliament provide, and what is the true reason for that remedy — construing the statute to suppress the mischief and advance the remedy. This 'mischief rule' was later applied by the Indian Supreme Court itself, notably in Bengal Immunity Co. v. State of Bihar (1955), and remains a core tool of statutory interpretation taught alongside the literal and golden rules." }
  ],
  "s1-tort:u2:2": [
    { name:"Radhey Shyam v. Gur Prasad Sarma", cite:"AIR 1978 All 86",
      facts:"Gur Prasad, who lived on the first floor of a building, sued his ground-floor neighbour Radhey Shyam to restrain him from installing and running a flour mill and oil expeller plant in his shop, alleging the rattling noise and vibration disturbed his family's peace and harmed their health.",
      holding:"The Allahabad High Court held that even in an already-noisy locality, a substantial new addition to noise/vibration that materially affects a neighbour's ordinary physical comfort amounts to actionable private nuisance — applying what is often called the 'Dhanna Lal rule.' A permanent injunction was granted restraining the mill's operation. Standard Indian case for teaching nuisance's 'substantial interference' requirement." }
  ],
  "s1-tort:u2:3": [
    { name:"Rudul Sah v. State of Bihar", cite:"AIR 1983 SC 1086; (1983) 4 SCC 141",
      facts:"Rudul Sah was acquitted by the Sessions Court, Muzaffarpur, Bihar on 3 June 1968, but was not released — he remained in illegal detention for over 14 more years, until 16 October 1982, with the State offering no coherent justification for the continued imprisonment.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court, on a habeas corpus petition under Article 32, ordered his release and — for the first time — awarded monetary compensation (₹35,000) directly in a writ petition for the violation of his Article 21 right to personal liberty through unlawful detention, rather than requiring him to file a separate civil suit. Landmark case establishing that compensation is an available public-law remedy for false imprisonment/illegal detention by the State, not just a private tort claim." }
  ],
  "s1-tort:u3:0": [
    { name:"D.P. Choudhary v. Kumari Manjulata", cite:"Rajasthan High Court, decided 4 April 1997",
      facts:"A local newspaper, Dainik Navjyoti, published a news item falsely claiming that 17-year-old Kumari Manjulata — a college student from a respected, well-educated family — had eloped with a boy at night, when she had in fact been attending night classes. The report was published negligently, without verifying the claim against reliable sources.",
      holding:"The Court held the publication was defamatory and actionable per se (without needing separate proof of actual financial loss), since it damaged Manjulata's reputation and marriage prospects; intention/malice is not required for civil defamation liability once a false, damaging statement is published. She was awarded ₹10,000 in general damages. Standard Indian case on newspaper/media liability for defamation and the 'actionable per se' principle for imputations affecting reputation and marriage prospects." }
  ],
  "s1-tort:u3:3": [
    { name:"Kasturilal Ralia Ram Jain v. State of Uttar Pradesh", cite:"AIR 1965 SC 1039; (1965) 1 SCR 375",
      facts:"Ralia Ram, a bullion trader, was taken into police custody in Meerut on suspicion of possessing stolen gold and silver. His goods were seized and kept in police custody; the silver was later returned on his release on bail, but the gold was never returned — a head constable had misappropriated it.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held the State was NOT liable: arrest, search and seizure of property are sovereign powers conferred on police by statute, and the negligence occurred while officers were exercising that sovereign power — distinguishing Vidyawati, where the negligent act (driving a jeep) was a non-sovereign function. Though widely criticised and narrowed by later cases (e.g. N. Nagendra Rao v. State of A.P., 1994), Kasturilal has never been expressly overruled and remains the classic case pairing with Vidyawati to teach the sovereign/non-sovereign functions distinction." }
  ],
  "s1-tort:u4:0": [
    { name:"Lucknow Development Authority v. M.K. Gupta", cite:"AIR 1994 SC 787; (1994) 1 SCC 243",
      facts:"M.K. Gupta had booked a flat under a housing scheme run by the Lucknow Development Authority (LDA), a statutory body. The LDA delayed possession and delivered defective, incomplete construction. The National Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission ruled against LDA; the authority appealed, arguing statutory development authorities were not 'service' providers amenable to the Consumer Protection Act, 1986.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held that housing construction and allotment is a 'service' under the Act, and that statutory/government bodies are just as amenable to consumer protection law as private builders — a government body cannot claim exemption merely because of its statutory character. Consumer fora can award compensation not just for the deficiency itself but for mental harassment caused by it. Foundational case for why the Consumer Protection Act, 2019 (successor to the 1986 Act) covers housing authorities and public bodies, not just private sellers." }
  ],
  "laws:u2:2": [
    { name:"Navtej Singh Johar & Ors. v. Union of India", cite:"AIR 2018 SC 4321",
      facts:"Section 377 of the IPC, a colonial-era provision, criminalised 'carnal intercourse against the order of nature', effectively outlawing consensual same-sex relations. It had a fraught history: struck down by the Delhi High Court in Naz Foundation (2009), reinstated by the Supreme Court in Suresh Kumar Koushal v. Naz Foundation (2013), which held only Parliament could change it. Dancer Navtej Singh Johar and four others filed a fresh petition after the Supreme Court's 2017 Puttaswamy judgment recognised privacy as a fundamental right.",
      holding:"A 5-judge Constitution Bench unanimously read down Section 377, decriminalising consensual same-sex conduct between adults, holding it violated Articles 14, 15, 19 and 21. The Court expressly overruled Koushal, noting that constitutional morality must prevail over majoritarian social morality, and that a provision's age or historical acceptance cannot immunise it from constitutional scrutiny once society's understanding of dignity and equality evolves. A textbook illustration of law as a 'mirror' that must adapt to (and sometimes lead) changing social values rather than freeze them." }
  ],
  "laws:u4:0": [
    { name:"Indra Sawhney v. Union of India", cite:"AIR 1993 SC 477 (the 'Mandal Commission case')",
      facts:"The Second Backward Classes Commission (the Mandal Commission, 1979) recommended 27% reservation for Other Backward Classes (OBCs) in central government jobs, on top of existing SC/ST reservation. When the government moved to implement this in 1990, it triggered nationwide protests. Indra Sawhney and others challenged the policy, arguing caste-based reservation violated the constitutional guarantee of equality and that backwardness should be assessed on economic grounds instead.",
      holding:"A 9-judge bench upheld caste as a valid indicator of social and educational backwardness, but introduced crucial limits: the 'creamy layer' (economically advanced members of a backward class) must be excluded from reservation benefits, total reservations cannot exceed 50% except in extraordinary circumstances, and reservation in promotions was held impermissible (though later modified by constitutional amendment). The foundational case for the legal framework governing affirmative action in Indian public employment." }
  ],
  "laws:u4:2": [
    { name:"M.C. Mehta v. State of Tamil Nadu", cite:"AIR 1997 SC 699; (1996) 6 SCC 756",
      facts:"Public interest lawyer M.C. Mehta filed a writ petition under Article 32 highlighting the widespread employment of children in the hazardous matchstick and fireworks factories of Sivakasi, Tamil Nadu, where a fatal factory accident killed 39 people and drew national attention to unsafe child labour conditions.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held that employing children in the hazardous manufacturing process itself was unconstitutional under Article 24, though children could still be employed in safer ancillary work like packing, kept away from the manufacturing area. The Court directed creation of a Child Labour Rehabilitation-cum-Welfare Fund funded by employer fines, mandated minimum wages for working children, and linked the ruling to Articles 39(e), 39(f), 41 and 47 as constitutional obligations toward child welfare, not just Article 24 in isolation." }
  ],
  "laws:u4:3": [
    { name:"Tuka Ram & Anr. v. State of Maharashtra (the 'Mathura Rape Case')", cite:"1979 AIR 185; (1979) 2 SCC 143",
      facts:"Mathura, a young tribal girl, was taken to Desai Ganj police station in Maharashtra in connection with a family dispute. Two police constables, Ganpat and Tukaram, sexually assaulted her while she was in custody at the station. The Sessions Court acquitted the accused, disbelieving her account; the Bombay High Court convicted them, holding that passive submission out of fear in a custodial setting cannot be treated as consent.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court reversed the High Court and acquitted the accused, reasoning that the absence of injury marks and resistance indicated consent — a decision that triggered nationwide outrage, including an open letter from law professors condemning the judgment's understanding of consent under custodial power imbalance. The public backlash directly led to the Criminal Law (Amendment) Act, 1983, which introduced specific custodial rape provisions, shifted the burden of proof onto the accused in custodial rape cases (Evidence Act, S.114-A), and mandated in-camera trials. Essential case for showing how a flawed judgment can itself become the catalyst for legal reform on violence against women." }
  ],
  "juris:u2:0": [
    { name:"Maneka Gandhi v. Union of India", cite:"AIR 1978 SC 597; (1978) 1 SCC 248",
      facts:"Journalist Maneka Gandhi's passport was impounded by the Regional Passport Officer 'in the interest of the general public', without giving her any reason or opportunity to be heard, under the Passport Act, 1967. She challenged this as arbitrary and violative of her fundamental rights.",
      holding:"A 7-judge bench held that 'procedure established by law' under Article 21 cannot be any procedure whatsoever — it must be fair, just and reasonable, effectively importing natural-justice/due-process style fairness into what had been read as a purely formal, positivist requirement in A.K. Gopalan (1950). The Court also held Articles 14, 19 and 21 are interlinked (the 'Golden Triangle'), not watertight compartments. This case is the classic illustration of Natural School reasoning displacing pure analytical positivism — validity of state action is judged not just by whether a procedure was 'enacted', but by whether it meets a substantive standard of fairness." }
  ],
  "juris:u2:3": [
    { name:"Vishaka & Ors. v. State of Rajasthan", cite:"AIR 1997 SC 3011; (1997) 6 SCC 241",
      facts:"Bhanwari Devi, a social worker under Rajasthan's Women Development Programme, was gang-raped in retaliation for trying to stop a child marriage. With the accused acquitted and no domestic law addressing workplace sexual harassment, women's rights groups filed a PIL under Article 32.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court read international conventions like CEDAW into the constitutional guarantee of gender equality and laid down binding workplace-harassment guidelines under Article 141, in the total absence of legislation. A textbook illustration of Pound's 'social engineering' and sociological jurisprudence — the Court treated law as a living tool for balancing social interests and filling real gaps, rather than confining itself to logically deriving results from existing rules (the analytical approach) or looking to historical custom (the historical approach)." }
  ],
  "juris:u3:2": [
    { name:"Yogendra Nath Naskar v. Commissioner of Income-Tax, Calcutta", cite:"AIR 1969 SC 1089; 1969 SCR (3) 742",
      facts:"The shebaits (managers) of two Hindu deities' dedicated estates challenged income-tax assessments raised directly against the deities as taxable 'individuals' under the Income Tax Act, 1922, arguing a deity cannot be a taxable unit since it is not a natural person.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held that a Hindu idol is a juristic entity capable of holding property, and can be taxed as an 'individual' through its shebaits, who manage its affairs — much like a guardian manages an infant's estate. The Court clarified this is a legal fiction: the juristic personality attaches to the deity as a symbolic representative of the purposes for which property was dedicated, not to the physical idol itself, and neither God nor any supernatural being can literally be a legal 'person'. The standard Indian case for teaching juristic (as opposed to natural) personality." }
  ],
  "juris:u3:3": [
    { name:"Salomon v. A. Salomon & Co. Ltd.", cite:"[1897] AC 22; [1896] UKHL 1 (foundational — basis of Indian company law's separate personality doctrine)",
      facts:"Aron Salomon, a sole-proprietor bootmaker, incorporated his business as a limited company, becoming its majority shareholder and largest secured creditor while his wife and children held the remaining shares. When the company went insolvent, the liquidator argued the company was merely Salomon's 'agent' or 'alias', and that he should be personally liable for its debts as if he had never incorporated at all.",
      holding:"The House of Lords unanimously held that a validly incorporated company is a separate legal person, distinct from its shareholders, regardless of how much control one shareholder holds — Salomon was not personally liable beyond his shareholding. This established the 'corporate veil' — the foundational fiction theory of corporate personality later adopted throughout Indian company law, distinguishing a corporation's juristic personality from that of the natural persons who own or run it." }
  ],
  "legal:u4:3": [
    { name:"Ashby v. White", cite:"(1703) 92 ER 126; 2 Ld Raym 938",
      facts:"Matthew Ashby, a qualified voter, was wrongfully prevented from voting by returning officer White. His preferred candidate won the election regardless, so Ashby suffered no measurable financial loss.",
      holding:"Chief Justice Holt held that the wrongful denial of a legal right is itself actionable even without actual damage — the direct origin of the maxim ubi jus ibi remedium ('where there is a right, there is a remedy'). The quintessential case for this exact maxim, taught wherever it appears in a syllabus." }
  ],
  "s3-crimes:u1:5": [
    { name:"M'Naghten's Case (foundational — basis of the insanity defense under BNS General Exceptions)", cite:"(1843) 10 Cl & Fin 200; 8 ER 718",
      facts:"Daniel M'Naghten, suffering from paranoid delusions that the Tory government was persecuting him, set out to kill Prime Minister Robert Peel. He mistakenly shot and killed Peel's secretary, Edward Drummond, instead. At trial, medical evidence showed his delusions had severely compromised his mental state.",
      holding:"The House of Lords laid down what became the 'M'Naghten Rule': a person is presumed sane, and to establish an insanity defence it must be clearly proved that at the time of the act, the accused was labouring under such a defect of reason from disease of the mind that they did not know the nature and quality of the act, or if they did know it, did not know it was wrong. This test remains the foundation for insanity as a general exception in Indian criminal law (analogous to the old IPC Section 84, now under BNS's General Exceptions)." },
    { name:"Darshan Singh v. State of Punjab & Anr.", cite:"AIR 2010 SC 1212; 2010 Cri LJ 1393",
      facts:"During a dispute over land partition between two brothers, one side struck the other's father, Bakhtawar Singh, with a gandasa (a heavy bladed farm tool), causing a severe head wound. Darshan Singh, the son, then fired his gun at the attacker, Gurcharan Singh, who died. Darshan Singh claimed he acted in exercise of his right of private defence to save his father's life.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court reinstated Darshan Singh's acquittal, holding that where there is a reasonable apprehension of death or grievous hurt, the right of private defence can extend even to causing the assailant's death — a person defending against imminent, serious violence is not expected to weigh the exact force needed 'in golden scales', and need not retreat. The accused only needs to show private defence is more probable than not, not prove it beyond reasonable doubt. The leading modern case comprehensively restating the scope and limits of private defence under Sections 96–106 (now under BNS's General Exceptions)." },
    { name:"State of Orissa v. Ram Bahadur Thapa", cite:"AIR 1960 Ori 161; 1960 Cri LJ 1349",
      facts:"A group including Ram Bahadur Thapa, a Nepalese man, visited an abandoned aerodrome at night to investigate local rumours that the site was haunted. Seeing a flickering light and moving figures in the dark, and genuinely believing he was seeing ghosts, Thapa attacked the figures with his kurki (a large knife), killing one woman and injuring others — who turned out to be local women collecting mahua flowers by lantern light, not supernatural beings.",
      holding:"The Orissa High Court upheld Thapa's acquittal, holding that his mistaken belief that he was attacking ghosts, not humans, was held in good faith under the circumstances (given the genuine local belief in the site's haunting), bringing his act within the 'mistake of fact' general exception (analogous to IPC S.79, now under BNS). Illustrates that even an objectively false and unusual belief can excuse criminal liability if it was honestly and reasonably held given the specific circumstances — a vivid teaching case for the boundaries of the mistake-of-fact defence." }
  ],
  "s3-crimes:u2:0": [
    { name:"Kehar Singh & Ors. v. State (Delhi Administration) ('the Indira Gandhi assassination case')", cite:"(1988) 3 SCC 609; AIR 1989 SC 1",
      facts:"Prime Minister Indira Gandhi was assassinated on 31 October 1984 by her own security guards, Beant Singh and Satwant Singh, in retaliation for Operation Blue Star. Kehar Singh was accused of being the mastermind who conspired with and provoked Beant Singh through inflammatory speeches, though he had no direct role in the shooting itself.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held that criminal conspiracy can be established through circumstantial evidence and inference from conduct — direct evidence of an explicit agreement is not essential, since conspiracies are by nature hatched in secrecy. Kehar Singh's conviction and death sentence were upheld under the 'rarest of rare' doctrine. The leading case for how courts prove the 'meeting of minds' required for criminal conspiracy without needing a smoking-gun agreement." },
    { name:"Yash Pal Mittal v. State of Punjab", cite:"1977 AIR 2433; (1977) 4 SCC 540",
      facts:"Yash Pal Mittal was charged with criminal conspiracy under Section 120-B (alongside charges of forging passports and cheating by personation against co-accused). He argued the trial was procedurally invalid, and separately that conspiracy required proof that all conspirators agreed to commit the exact same specific illegal act.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held that the very agreement, concert, or league is itself the gist of the offence of criminal conspiracy — the illegal act need not actually be carried out, and it is not necessary that every conspirator agree to a single identical act; there can be plurality of means and even devices unknown to some conspirators, so long as there is unity of object or purpose. Clarifies that criminal conspiracy is complete the moment the unlawful agreement is formed, regardless of what happens afterward." },
    { name:"State (NCT of Delhi) v. Navjot Sandhu @ Afsan Guru (the 'Parliament Attack case')", cite:"AIR 2005 SC 3820; (2005) 11 SCC 600",
      facts:"On 13 December 2001, five armed terrorists attacked the Indian Parliament complex; all five were killed in the ensuing gun battle along with several security personnel. Investigators arrested Mohd. Afzal Guru, Shaukat Hussain, S.A.R. Gilani, and Navjot Sandhu, relying heavily on circumstantial evidence — mobile phone records, recovered hideouts, and seized electronics — to establish a wider conspiracy behind the attack.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held that a conspiracy conviction can rest entirely on circumstantial evidence forming an unbroken, coherent chain — call records, recoveries, and conduct sufficiently proved Afzal Guru's participation in the conspiracy, upholding his death sentence, while insufficient evidence led to Gilani and Navjot Sandhu's acquittal on the conspiracy charge itself. Demonstrates both how far circumstantial-evidence-based conspiracy convictions can reach, and their limits where the chain of evidence has real gaps." }
  ],
  "s3-crimes:u2:1": [
    { name:"Kedar Nath Singh v. State of Bihar", cite:"AIR 1962 SC 955",
      facts:"Kedar Nath Singh, a member of the Forward Communist Party, delivered a speech strongly criticising the ruling Congress government and calling for revolution. He was convicted of sedition under Section 124A IPC. He challenged the constitutionality of the sedition provision itself, arguing it violated the fundamental right to freedom of speech under Article 19(1)(a).",
      holding:"The Supreme Court upheld the constitutionality of the sedition provision but significantly narrowed its scope: only speech that incites violence or public disorder amounts to sedition — mere strong criticism, disapproval, or 'disaffection' toward the government, without incitement to violence, is protected free speech and cannot be punished as sedition. The controlling precedent for interpreting offences against the State narrowly, in line with free-speech protections, and still cited in debates over BNS's reformulated provisions on this offence." },
    { name:"Balwant Singh & Anr. v. State of Punjab", cite:"(1995) 3 SCC 214; 1995 SCR (1) 411",
      facts:"On the day Prime Minister Indira Gandhi was assassinated, two government employees, Balwant Singh and Bhupinder Singh, were arrested near a cinema in Chandigarh for shouting pro-Khalistan slogans a few times in a public market, amid heightened communal tension. They were convicted of sedition and promoting enmity.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court acquitted both men, holding that casually raising slogans a couple of times, without any accompanying disruptive conduct, procession, or actual response/reaction from the crowd, did not amount to incitement to violence or public disorder — applying Kedar Nath Singh's requirement that mere words, however provocative in isolation, are not sedition without a real link to inciting violence. Reinforces that context and actual effect on public order, not just the content of the words themselves, determine whether speech crosses into sedition." },
    { name:"Vinod Dua v. Union of India", cite:"(2021) SCC OnLine SC 414",
      facts:"Journalist Vinod Dua was booked for sedition (S.124A) and public mischief (S.505(b)) over a YouTube show criticising the Prime Minister's handling of the COVID-19 crisis and the migrant workers' hardship in 2020, after a complainant alleged his remarks caused panic and could incite food riots.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court quashed the FIR, reiterating and applying Kedar Nath Singh's test in the digital/press-freedom context — a journalist's criticism of government policy, however sharp, is not sedition unless it incites violence or public disorder, and the right to criticise government measures is part of the core of free political speech under Article 19(1)(a). A modern application confirming Kedar Nath Singh's limits remain fully binding even in cases against journalists over online content." }
  ],
  "s3-crimes:u3:1": [
    { name:"Sarla Mudgal v. Union of India", cite:"AIR 1995 SC 1531; (1995) 3 SCC 635",
      facts:"Several Hindu husbands, still validly married under Hindu law, converted to Islam solely in order to solemnise a second marriage without divorcing their first wives — since Islamic personal law permits polygamy. Their abandoned first wives sought prosecution of their husbands for bigamy.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held that conversion to another religion does not automatically dissolve a valid Hindu marriage — it subsists until validly dissolved under the Hindu Marriage Act, 1955. A second marriage contracted after such conversion, without dissolving the first, constitutes bigamy. Landmark case for offences relating to marriage." },
    { name:"Bhaurao Shankar Lokhande & Anr. v. State of Maharashtra", cite:"AIR 1965 SC 1564",
      facts:"Bhaurao Lokhande, already married to Indubai under proper Hindu ceremonies, went through a second marriage ceremony with Kamlabai while Indubai was still alive. He was convicted of bigamy, but argued the prosecution failed to prove his second marriage had been performed with the essential ceremonies required for a valid Hindu marriage.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held that for a bigamy conviction, the prosecution must prove the second marriage was validly 'solemnized' — meaning the essential ceremonies (such as invocation before the sacred fire and saptapadi, where custom requires them) were actually performed. Since these were not proved here, the second marriage was not a valid marriage at all, so the bigamy charge failed — merely going through some ceremony intending to be seen as married does not itself satisfy legal 'solemnization'. Illustrates a real gap in bigamy prosecutions: a fraudulently incomplete 'marriage' can paradoxically escape bigamy liability precisely because it isn't valid enough to count as a second marriage." },
    { name:"Lily Thomas v. Union of India", cite:"AIR 2000 SC 1650",
      facts:"After the Sarla Mudgal ruling, some men and commentators sought a review, arguing the decision infringed the freedom of religion under Article 25 by effectively penalising conversion, and violated Article 21 by exposing converts to prosecution. Sushmita Ghosh's case (whose husband converted to Islam to remarry) was among those reconsidered.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court dismissed the review petitions and reaffirmed Sarla Mudgal in full: converting religion solely to escape the first marriage's bigamy prohibition, without genuine faith, does not fall within Article 25's protection, and lawful prosecution for bigamy does not violate Article 21. Confirmed that a second marriage contracted this way remains void and punishable under the bigamy provisions, closing off further challenges to the Sarla Mudgal principle." }
  ],
  "s3-crimes:u3:3": [
    { name:"R v. Prince (widely cited in Indian jurisprudence)", cite:"(1875) LR 2 CCR 154",
      facts:"Prince took an unmarried girl under 16 out of her father's possession without consent, honestly and reasonably believing she was 18 based on her appearance and her own statement.",
      holding:"The court held Prince guilty regardless of his honest belief about her age, because the underlying act — taking a girl out of her father's lawful possession without consent — was itself wrongful. Illustrates why 'mistake of fact' is a narrower defence than commonly assumed for offences against children." },
    { name:"Independent Thought v. Union of India", cite:"(2017) 10 SCC 800; AIR 2017 SC 4904",
      facts:"Exception 2 to Section 375 IPC (the rape provision) exempted a man from rape liability for non-consensual sex with his own wife, so long as she was 15 or older — even though the general age of consent for sex was 18. A child-rights organisation, Independent Thought, challenged this exception as unconstitutional to the extent it applied to wives between 15 and 18.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court struck down Exception 2 as unconstitutional for wives under 18, holding that the marital-status exception created an irrational classification between married and unmarried minor girls, violating Articles 14, 15, and 21 — sexual intercourse with a wife below 18 now constitutes rape, harmonising the rape provision with the POCSO Act's protection of all children under 18 regardless of marital status." },
    { name:"Sakshi v. Union of India", cite:"AIR 2004 SC 3566; Writ Petition (Crl.) 33 of 1997",
      facts:"An NGO, Sakshi, filed a PIL under Article 32 seeking a broader judicial interpretation of 'sexual intercourse' under the rape provision (S.375 IPC), to cover forms of penetration beyond penile-vaginal intercourse — arguing the narrow definition left many child sexual abuse victims without a rape-level remedy, since such acts were only prosecutable as lesser offences (outraging modesty, unnatural offences).",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held that expanding the substantive definition of rape was a matter for Parliament, not judicial interpretation, and declined to read the provision more broadly itself — but it did lay down important procedural protections for child victims during trial, including screens preventing the child from seeing the accused, written cross-examination questions routed through the judge, and permitted breaks during testimony. Illustrates the judiciary's self-imposed limits on rewriting substantive criminal definitions even while actively protecting child witnesses procedurally — Parliament later substantially addressed the underlying gap through the POCSO Act, 2012." }
  ],
  "s3-crimes:u3:4": [
    { name:"K.M. Nanavati v. State of Maharashtra", cite:"AIR 1962 SC 605",
      facts:"Naval Commander K.M. Nanavati shot and killed his wife's lover, Prem Ahuja, after his wife confessed to the affair. He claimed the killing occurred in a sudden fit of passion after confronting Ahuja and demanding to know if he would marry his wife and take responsibility for their children.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held that the gap between the confession and the shooting — Nanavati dropped his family at a cinema, went to his ship for a pistol, then drove to Ahuja's house — showed a 'cooling-off' period, so the killing was not committed in the heat of the moment, and the defence of 'grave and sudden provocation' (reducing murder to culpable homicide) failed. The standard case for the boundary between murder and culpable homicide not amounting to murder under offences affecting life." },
    { name:"Virsa Singh v. State of Punjab", cite:"AIR 1958 SC 465; 1958 SCR 1495",
      facts:"During a scuffle, Virsa Singh struck Khem Singh a single severe blow with a spear, which proved fatal. Virsa Singh argued he only intended to cause bodily injury, not death, and so should be convicted of culpable homicide not amounting to murder rather than murder.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held that under the murder provision's 'thirdly' clause (BNS successor to IPC S.300 thirdly), the prosecution need not prove the accused intended to cause death at all — it is enough to prove the accused intended to cause the specific bodily injury found, and that injury was objectively sufficient, in the ordinary course of nature, to cause death. This 'four-fold test' from Virsa Singh remains the standard framework for distinguishing murder from culpable homicide wherever only bodily-injury intent (not death intent) is in question." },
    { name:"Reg v. Govinda", cite:"(1877) ILR 1 Bom 342",
      facts:"An 18-year-old man, Govinda, kicked and struck his 15-year-old wife with his fist during a domestic assault; he then knelt on her chest and struck her face two or three times, one blow landing on her eye, causing a brain haemorrhage from which she died shortly after. The trial court convicted him of murder.",
      holding:"The Bombay High Court (Justice Melvill) reduced the conviction to culpable homicide not amounting to murder, using this case to systematically explain the structural difference between Sections 299 (culpable homicide) and 300 (murder) — the offence is culpable homicide where there is intention to cause bodily injury likely to cause death, but escalates to murder only where there is intention to cause death, or intention to cause a bodily injury objectively sufficient in the ordinary course of nature to cause death, or knowledge that the act is so imminently dangerous it must in all probability cause death. The foundational teaching case for the Section 299/300 distinction, still used to explain the graduated structure of homicide offences under the BNS." }
  ],
  "s3-crimes:u4:0": [
    { name:"Pyare Lal Bhargava v. State of Rajasthan", cite:"AIR 1963 SC 1094; 1963 Supp (1) SCR 689",
      facts:"Pyare Lal, a Superintendent in the Chief Engineer's office, took an official file home overnight without authorisation to show it to an acquaintance, then returned the file to the office the next day. He argued this could not be 'theft' since he intended to return it.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held that theft is complete the moment property is dishonestly moved out of another's possession without consent, regardless of whether the taker later returns it — even a temporary, unauthorised removal satisfies the definition. 'Dishonest intention' for theft does not require an intention to permanently keep the property." },
    { name:"K.N. Mehra v. State of Rajasthan", cite:"AIR 1957 SC 369",
      facts:"Two Indian Air Force cadets, K.N. Mehra and Phillips, took off in a Harvard aircraft without authorisation — flying a different aircraft than assigned, before their scheduled time, without completing mandatory flight paperwork — and flew to Pakistan, later claiming it was accidental due to bad weather.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held their unauthorised flight constituted theft of the aircraft: since the flight was unauthorised, there was no valid consent, and taking temporary but unauthorised use of the aircraft for their own purposes, depriving the Government of its use, satisfied 'dishonest intention' — theft under Indian law, unlike English larceny, does not require an intention to permanently deprive the owner; temporary wrongful deprivation suffices." },
    { name:"Ram Ratan alias Ratan Ahir & Ors. v. State of Bihar & Anr.", cite:"AIR 1965 SC 926; 1965 SCR (1) 293",
      facts:"Cattle belonging to the complainants strayed onto a field claimed by Ram Ratan and others, who seized the cattle to impound them under the Cattle Trespass Act, 1871 (which permits seizure of trespassing cattle). The cattle owners tried to forcibly rescue their animals, and a violent clash broke out in which one person died. The seizers were charged with theft/robbery of the cattle.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held that seizing cattle under a bona fide, even if ultimately mistaken, claim of right under the Cattle Trespass Act did not amount to theft, since the accused lacked 'dishonest intention' — they genuinely believed they were acting within a lawful statutory power, not wrongfully depriving the true owner of property they knew belonged to someone else. Illustrates the boundary of theft from the opposite direction to Pyare Lal Bhargava and K.N. Mehra: taking another's property is not theft at all if done under a genuine, good-faith claim of legal right, since dishonest intention is theft's essential mental element." }
  ],
  "s3-const:u1:2": [
    { name:"S.R. Bommai v. Union of India", cite:"AIR 1994 SC 1918; (1994) 3 SCC 1",
      facts:"Karnataka Chief Minister S.R. Bommai's government was dismissed via President's Rule (Article 356) after the Governor reported Bommai had lost majority support due to defections — without ever allowing a floor test in the Assembly to verify this. Similar arbitrary dismissals had occurred in several other states for apparent political reasons.",
      holding:"A 9-judge bench held that the President's satisfaction under Article 356 is not immune from judicial review, and that a floor test on the Assembly floor — not the Governor's subjective assessment — is the proper way to test whether a government has lost majority support. The Court held federalism and secularism are part of the Constitution's basic structure. The foundational case for understanding India's 'quasi-federal' structure — neither purely federal nor purely unitary — and for curbing the misuse of President's Rule to dismiss elected state governments." },
    { name:"State of West Bengal v. Union of India", cite:"AIR 1963 SC 1241",
      facts:"The Union enacted the Coal Bearing Areas (Acquisition and Development) Act, 1957, to acquire coal-bearing land in West Bengal for national development. West Bengal challenged this as beyond Parliament's power, arguing India's states possess an inherent sovereignty of their own — like the constituent states of a 'true' federation such as the USA — meaning the Union could not simply legislate to acquire state-owned property.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held that, unlike a fully federal constitution formed by fully sovereign states surrendering some powers upward, the Indian Constitution creates a Union where states have no independent sovereignty of their own — they are creations of the Constitution, not pre-existing sovereign entities that federated together, and Parliament can validly legislate to acquire state property for purposes within its competence. Establishes India as a strong-Centre, 'quasi-federal' Union rather than a classical federation — a position later balanced (not overturned) by S.R. Bommai's recognition of federalism as part of the basic structure; the two cases are typically taught together to show the tension between centralising and federalising strands in Indian constitutional law." },
    { name:"Kesavananda Bharati v. State of Kerala", cite:"AIR 1973 SC 1461",
      facts:"Land reform legislation restricting a Kerala mutt's property rights became the vehicle for a 13-judge bench to determine whether there were any limits on Parliament's power to amend the Constitution, including the features that structure India's federal-unitary balance.",
      holding:"The Court held Parliament can amend any part of the Constitution but not its 'basic structure' — and federalism was among the features later confirmed (in S.R. Bommai) to be part of that basic structure. Completes the federalism trilogy: State of West Bengal establishes India's states have no inherent sovereignty; Kesavananda establishes that whatever federal balance the Constitution does strike cannot be amended away entirely; Bommai confirms federalism specifically is protected as part of the basic structure." }
  ],
  "s3-const:u2:1": [
    { name:"Ajay Hasia v. Khalid Mujib Sehravardi", cite:"AIR 1981 SC 487; (1981) 1 SCC 722",
      facts:"Students challenged the admission process at a Regional Engineering College in Srinagar (managed by a registered society) as arbitrary, since a huge weightage was given to a brief, subjective viva voce interview. Before reaching the merits, the Court first had to decide whether the college — technically a private society, not a government department — could even be treated as 'State' under Article 12, since fundamental rights can only be enforced against the State.",
      holding:"The Court held that a body is 'State' under Article 12 if it is an instrumentality or agency of government, laying down a six-factor test (building on R.D. Shetty v. International Airport Authority) — including government control over the entity's finances, functions, and management. The leading case for the 'instrumentality or agency' test used to determine whether a body counts as 'other authorities' under Article 12, expanding fundamental-rights accountability well beyond formal government departments." },
    { name:"Ramana Dayaram Shetty v. International Airport Authority of India", cite:"AIR 1979 SC 1628",
      facts:"The International Airport Authority invited tenders for a restaurant at Bombay airport, setting a mandatory eligibility condition requiring five years' experience as a registered second-class hotelier. The tender was nonetheless accepted from a bidder who did not meet this condition. Ramana Dayaram Shetty, who had not himself submitted a tender, challenged the arbitrary deviation from the Authority's own published eligibility criteria.",
      holding:"Justice Bhagwati held the Authority was 'State' under Article 12 given complete government control over its composition, finances, and policy — and, having publicly set an eligibility standard, the Authority was bound by its own norm and could not arbitrarily waive it for one bidder, violating Article 14. This is the true origin of the 'instrumentality or agency' test that Ajay Hasia later refined into six factors — Ramana Shetty is the foundational case, Ajay Hasia the more systematised sequel, and the two are almost always taught as a pair for the evolution of the 'State' test under Article 12." },
    { name:"Pradeep Kumar Biswas v. Indian Institute of Chemical Biology", cite:"(2002) 5 SCC 111",
      facts:"Employees of a CSIR (Council of Scientific and Industrial Research) research institute challenged the termination of their services, but the Calcutta High Court refused to entertain their writ petition, following an earlier Supreme Court ruling (Sabhajit Tewary, 1975) that CSIR was not 'State' under Article 12. A larger 7-judge bench was constituted to reconsider whether that 27-year-old precedent should still stand, given how the 'State' test had evolved through Ramana Shetty and Ajay Hasia since then.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court expressly overruled Sabhajit Tewary, holding CSIR was 'State' under Article 12 given the government's 'deep and pervasive control' over its finances, functions, and administration — refining the test to focus less on a mechanical checklist of factors and more on whether the body is really an instrumentality of government in substance. Marks the modern, final stage of the 'State' test's evolution: Ramana Shetty → Ajay Hasia → Pradeep Kumar Biswas is the standard three-case sequence for tracing how the courts progressively expanded and then refined who counts as 'State'." }
  ],
  "s3-const:u2:2": [
    { name:"Bhikaji Narain Dhakras v. State of Madhya Pradesh", cite:"AIR 1955 SC 781; 1955 SCR (2) 58",
      facts:"A 1947 amendment to the Motor Vehicles Act gave the government a monopoly over motor transport, excluding private operators. When the Constitution came into force in 1950, private operators argued the amendment was void as inconsistent with their fundamental right to carry on trade/business under Article 19(1)(g).",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held that a pre-Constitution law inconsistent with fundamental rights is not wiped out entirely — it becomes merely dormant/unenforceable ('eclipsed') to the extent of the inconsistency, and can spring back to full force if the inconsistency is later removed (e.g. by constitutional amendment). This is the origin case for the 'Doctrine of Eclipse' under Article 13, distinguishing pre-Constitution laws (eclipsed, revivable) from post-Constitution laws inconsistent with fundamental rights (void from inception, not revivable)." },
    { name:"Keshavan Madhava Menon v. State of Bombay", cite:"AIR 1951 SC 128",
      facts:"In 1949, before the Constitution came into force, Menon published a pamphlet without prior authorisation, violating the pre-Constitution Indian Press (Emergency Powers) Act, 1931. He was still being prosecuted for this after 26 January 1950, when the Constitution — and the fundamental right to free speech under Article 19(1)(a) — came into effect. He argued the prosecution should be dropped since the underlying law was now inconsistent with his fundamental rights.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held that Article 13(1) operates only prospectively, from the date the Constitution came into force — it does not retrospectively wipe out actions, offences, or liabilities that had already validly arisen under a pre-Constitution law before that date. His prosecution, having been validly initiated before the Constitution, could continue. This is the very case that established the eclipse concept a few years before Bhikaji Narain Dhakras applied and named it more fully — together they establish that pre-Constitution inconsistent laws are only prospectively void, not retrospectively erased." },
    { name:"Deep Chand v. State of Uttar Pradesh", cite:"AIR 1959 SC 648",
      facts:"After the Constitution came into force, Uttar Pradesh passed the U.P. Transport Service (Development) Act, 1955, nationalising motor transport in a manner that conflicted with the central Motor Vehicles Act. Bus operators, already lawfully running services under central law, challenged the state Act as inconsistent with their fundamental right to carry on trade under Article 19(1)(g), and also void for repugnancy with central legislation.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held that a post-Constitution law inconsistent with fundamental rights is void ab initio — a 'still-born' law, void from the very moment it is enacted — unlike a pre-Constitution law, which is merely eclipsed and can later revive. Since the Doctrine of Eclipse presupposes a law that was once fully valid and only later became inconsistent, it has no application to a law that was invalid the instant it was made. This is the essential contrasting case to Bhikaji Narain Dhakras: eclipse for pre-Constitution laws, void ab initio for post-Constitution ones — though later softened somewhat in State of Gujarat v. Ambica Mills (1974) as regards non-citizens." }
  ],
  "s3-const:u3:0": [
    { name:"Shreya Singhal v. Union of India", cite:"AIR 2015 SC 1523; (2015) 5 SCC 1",
      facts:"Two young women were arrested by Mumbai police under Section 66A of the Information Technology Act, 2000 merely for posting a Facebook comment questioning a city-wide shutdown after a political leader's death. Section 66A criminalised sending 'grossly offensive' or 'menacing' information online, without clearly defining those terms. Law student Shreya Singhal filed a PIL challenging the provision's constitutionality.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court struck down Section 66A in its entirety, holding it unconstitutionally vague and overbroad, capable of covering everything from mere annoyance to genuine threats, with no principled way to distinguish protected speech from punishable speech — creating an unacceptable 'chilling effect' on free expression. The landmark case establishing that online/digital speech gets the same Article 19(1)(a) protection as traditional speech, and that a restriction failing to fit within Article 19(2)'s specific grounds cannot survive, however well-intentioned." },
    { name:"Romesh Thappar v. State of Madras", cite:"1950 AIR 124; 1950 SCR 594",
      facts:"Romesh Thappar, editor of the political journal 'Cross Roads', had its entry and circulation banned in Madras State under the Madras Maintenance of Public Order Act, 1949, on the ground of maintaining public order/safety — even though, at that time, Article 19(2) did not list 'public order' as a valid ground for restricting speech (it only listed grounds like security of the State).",
      holding:"The Supreme Court, in one of its earliest constitutional rulings, struck down the ban, holding that freedom of speech includes freedom of circulation — publication without circulation is meaningless — and that restrictions must fit strictly within the specific grounds actually listed in Article 19(2), not a broader 'public order' rationale the Constitution didn't yet recognise. This judgment (and a companion case decided the same day) directly triggered the First Constitutional Amendment, 1951, which added 'public order' as a ground precisely because the government wanted to reverse this outcome — illustrating how an Article 19 ruling can itself reshape the Constitution's text." },
    { name:"Bennett Coleman & Co. v. Union of India", cite:"AIR 1973 SC 106",
      facts:"The government's Newsprint Policy, 1972-73 directly regulated the page count and circulation of newspapers by controlling newsprint import quotas, ostensibly to manage a newsprint shortage. Major newspaper proprietors, including Bennett Coleman & Co., argued this was really a disguised, indirect restriction on the press's ability to publish and circulate freely.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held that press freedom under Article 19(1)(a) has both a qualitative dimension (what can be published) and a quantitative dimension (how much can be published/circulated) — restricting the volume of newsprint a paper can use, even framed as 'resource allocation', is itself a restriction on speech if not genuinely justified by an actual shortage. Struck down the Newsprint Policy's quantitative limits as an unreasonable, disguised restriction on press freedom, extending Romesh Thappar's circulation principle to indirect, resource-based restrictions on the press." }
  ],
  "s3-const:u3:3": [
    { name:"D.K. Basu v. State of West Bengal", cite:"(1997) 1 SCC 416; AIR 1997 SC 610",
      facts:"D.K. Basu, Executive Chairman of Legal Aid Services West Bengal, wrote a letter to the Chief Justice of India in 1986 highlighting newspaper reports of custodial deaths and torture in police lock-ups across India, with no clear guidelines governing how police should treat persons under arrest. The letter was treated as a writ petition.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held custodial torture and death violate the Article 21 right to life and dignity, and issued 11 mandatory guidelines for arrest and detention — including preparing an arrest memo witnessed by an independent person, informing a friend/relative of the arrest, permitting medical examination, and producing the arrestee before a magistrate within 24 hours. These guidelines were later written into the CrPC (Sections 41A–41D) and remain the baseline safeguards under Article 22's protection against arbitrary arrest and detention." },
    { name:"Joginder Kumar v. State of U.P.", cite:"1994 AIR 1349; (1994) 4 SCC 260",
      facts:"Joginder Kumar, a young law graduate and newly enrolled advocate, was summoned by the Senior Superintendent of Police, Ghaziabad, for questioning in an inquiry. He appeared voluntarily but was then detained without any formal arrest being recorded, and his family was not informed of his whereabouts for several days, prompting a habeas corpus petition under Article 32.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held that a police officer's power to arrest does not mean arrest is mandatory or routine whenever an offence is alleged — arrest must be justified by reasonable satisfaction, after some investigation, that it is genuinely necessary, not merely because it is legally permissible. The Court issued guidelines requiring that an arrested person's friend/relative be informed and this be recorded in the police diary. This predates and directly lays the groundwork for D.K. Basu's more comprehensive guidelines three years later — the two are the standard sequential pair for Article 22's protection against arbitrary arrest." },
    { name:"A.K. Roy v. Union of India", cite:"AIR 1982 SC 710",
      facts:"A.K. Roy, a Marxist Member of Parliament, was preventively detained under the newly promulgated National Security Act, 1980, on the ground that his activities threatened public order — without a formal trial, based purely on the executive's assessment of future risk. He challenged the ordinance-making power used to create the Act and the Act's preventive detention provisions as unconstitutionally vague and violative of Articles 14, 21 and 22.",
      holding:"A 5-judge bench upheld the National Security Act's validity, confirming that preventive detention itself is constitutionally permitted under Article 22(3)-(7) (unlike ordinary criminal procedure, it can dispense with a full trial and legal representation) — but held that even preventive detention must still satisfy Maneka Gandhi's 'fair, just and reasonable' standard, and that Article 21 is never fully suspended. Essential case for the specific sub-category of Article 22 dealing with preventive detention (as opposed to D.K. Basu/Joginder Kumar's focus on ordinary criminal arrest) — the constitutionally distinct, more permissive regime Article 22(3) carves out for detention without trial." }
  ],
  "s3-const:u5:1": [
    { name:"Minerva Mills Ltd. v. Union of India", cite:"AIR 1980 SC 1789; (1980) 3 SCC 625",
      facts:"During the Emergency (1975-77), the 42nd Constitutional Amendment inserted provisions giving Directive Principles of State Policy (Part IV) absolute primacy over Fundamental Rights under Articles 14 and 19 (Part III), and barred courts from reviewing constitutional amendments at all. Minerva Mills, taken over by the government under a law claimed to implement DPSPs, challenged this scheme.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court struck down these provisions, holding that the harmonious balance between Fundamental Rights and Directive Principles is itself part of the Constitution's basic structure — DPSPs can inform how Fundamental Rights are implemented, but cannot be given blanket primacy that guts Part III entirely. The Court also held that judicial review itself is part of the basic structure and cannot be excluded even by constitutional amendment. Along with Kesavananda Bharati, the essential case for understanding how Fundamental Rights and Directive Principles are meant to work together, not in a strict hierarchy." },
    { name:"I.C. Golaknath v. State of Punjab", cite:"1967 AIR 1643; 1967 SCR (2) 762",
      facts:"The Golaknath family, large landowners in Punjab, challenged the 17th Constitutional Amendment, which had placed the Punjab Security of Land Tenures Act, 1953 (a land-ceiling law) into the Ninth Schedule to shield it from judicial review for violating their property and equality rights under Part III.",
      holding:"By a narrow majority, the Supreme Court held that a constitutional amendment is 'law' within the meaning of Article 13(2), and Parliament therefore has NO power to amend or abridge any Fundamental Right at all — Part III was placed effectively beyond Parliament's amending reach. This extreme, rigid position (later replaced by Kesavananda Bharati's more balanced 'basic structure' compromise, which allows FR amendment short of destroying the basic structure) is essential background for understanding why the FR-DPSP relationship needed judicial recalibration: Golaknath made FR absolute, Kesavananda made them amendable-but-protected, and Minerva Mills then fixed the correct balance specifically against DPSP overreach." },
    { name:"Kesavananda Bharati v. State of Kerala", cite:"AIR 1973 SC 1461",
      facts:"Land reform legislation restricting a Kerala mutt's property rights became the vehicle for a 13-judge bench to resolve the tension left open by Golaknath: can Parliament amend Fundamental Rights at all, and if so, are there any limits?",
      holding:"The Court overruled Golaknath's rigid position, holding Parliament CAN amend Fundamental Rights, but cannot alter the Constitution's 'basic structure' — creating the middle path between total FR rigidity (Golaknath) and unlimited amending power. This is the doctrinal foundation Minerva Mills later built on to specifically strike down DPSP-primacy provisions: the basic structure includes the FR-DPSP balance itself, so even a constitutional amendment favouring DPSPs cannot destroy Fundamental Rights wholesale." }
  ],
  "s3-const:u2:4": [
    { name:"Indra Sawhney v. Union of India", cite:"AIR 1993 SC 477 (the 'Mandal Commission case')",
      facts:"The Mandal Commission recommended 27% reservation for Other Backward Classes (OBCs) in central government jobs, on top of existing SC/ST reservation. When the government moved to implement this in 1990, it was challenged as violating the equality guarantee.",
      holding:"A 9-judge bench upheld caste as a valid indicator of backwardness for reservation, but introduced limits: the 'creamy layer' must be excluded, and total reservations generally cannot exceed 50%. The foundational case for applying Article 15/16's protective discrimination provisions in practice." },
    { name:"M. Nagaraj v. Union of India", cite:"(2006) 8 SCC 212; AIR 2007 SC 71",
      facts:"Following Indra Sawhney, Parliament passed constitutional amendments (77th, 81st, 82nd, 85th) inserting Articles 16(4A) and 16(4B) to permit reservation in promotions (not just initial appointment) for SCs/STs, and allowing unfilled reserved vacancies to be carried forward to future years. These amendments were challenged as violating the basic structure by undermining the equality code and departing from Indra Sawhney's framework.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court upheld the amendments as constitutionally valid, but held that if a State chooses to provide reservation in promotions, it must first collect quantifiable data showing the class's backwardness and inadequate representation, and must still respect the 50% ceiling and exclude the 'creamy layer' — even for SCs/STs. Extends Indra Sawhney's safeguards specifically to the more contentious question of promotion-based reservation, showing how the basic structure doctrine constrains even constitutional amendments on reservation policy." },
    { name:"Janhit Abhiyan v. Union of India", cite:"2022 SCC OnLine SC 1540",
      facts:"The 103rd Constitutional Amendment, 2019 inserted Articles 15(6) and 16(6), introducing a 10% reservation for Economically Weaker Sections (EWS) in education and public employment, purely on economic grounds — while expressly excluding SC/ST/OBC citizens even if they were also economically weak, since they already benefited from other reservation categories. Petitioners argued this breached the 50% ceiling set in Indra Sawhney and used an impermissible sole criterion (economic status) unconnected to social/educational backwardness.",
      holding:"By a 3:2 majority, the Supreme Court upheld the EWS reservation, holding that reservation based purely on economic criteria does not violate the basic structure, and that excluding SC/ST/OBC citizens from this specific EWS quota (since they have separate reservation channels) was not itself discriminatory. The dissenting judges disagreed, arguing the exclusion violated the equality code. Shows the Indra Sawhney 50% ceiling and 'backwardness' framework being tested against a fundamentally different, economics-only basis for reservation, decades after the original caste-based framework was set." }
  ],
  "s3-const:u3:2": [
    { name:"Maneka Gandhi v. Union of India", cite:"AIR 1978 SC 597; (1978) 1 SCC 248",
      facts:"Journalist Maneka Gandhi's passport was impounded without any hearing or stated reason, 'in the interest of the general public', under the Passport Act, 1967.",
      holding:"A 7-judge bench held 'procedure established by law' under Article 21 must itself be fair, just and reasonable — not merely any procedure enacted by statute — overruling the narrower view in A.K. Gopalan (1950) and holding Articles 14, 19 and 21 are interlinked (the 'Golden Triangle'). The essential case for the modern, expansive reading of Article 21." },
    { name:"Olga Tellis & Ors. v. Bombay Municipal Corporation", cite:"1985 SCC (3) 545; AIR 1986 SC 180",
      facts:"The Maharashtra government and Bombay Municipal Corporation decided to evict pavement and slum dwellers from Bombay's streets, including during the monsoon season, without notice or resettlement — most of these dwellers were rural migrants who had built makeshift homes on pavements out of sheer economic necessity. Journalist Olga Tellis and others petitioned on their behalf, though the dwellers had earlier conceded before the High Court that they claimed no fundamental right to occupy pavements.",
      holding:"A 5-judge bench held that the right to life under Article 21 includes the right to livelihood, since eviction from one's only means of accessing work effectively deprives a person of the means to live — depriving someone of their livelihood is a form of depriving them of life itself. However, the Court still upheld the Corporation's power to evict pavement encroachments (since pavements are meant for passage), requiring only that eviction follow a fair procedure, with humane treatment and avoiding the monsoon season. The essential case for how Article 21 has been read to encompass social and economic rights (livelihood, shelter, dignity) beyond mere physical existence, even where the underlying property claim itself fails." },
    { name:"Francis Coralie Mullin v. Administrator, Union Territory of Delhi", cite:"1981 AIR 746; 1981 SCR (2) 516",
      facts:"Francis Coralie Mullin, a British national, was detained under the COFEPOSA Act, 1974 (a preventive detention law targeting smuggling/foreign exchange offences). While in custody, restrictive conditions meant she could see her young daughter and sister only once a month, and could meet her lawyer only with prior permission from the District Magistrate and in the presence of a customs officer.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held these restrictions violated Articles 14 and 21, holding that the right to life is not confined to mere animal existence — it includes the right to live with human dignity and access the bare necessities that make dignified life possible, such as adequate nutrition, shelter, and — central to this case — meaningful contact with family and counsel, even for a person in preventive detention. Extends Maneka Gandhi's 'fair, just and reasonable' procedure requirement specifically into the dignity of detained persons, confirming that even lawful detention cannot strip away every basic human need." }
  ],
  "s3-const:u3:4": [
    { name:"M.C. Mehta v. State of Tamil Nadu", cite:"AIR 1997 SC 699; (1996) 6 SCC 756",
      facts:"A PIL highlighted widespread child employment in Sivakasi's hazardous matchstick and fireworks factories, after a fatal factory accident killed 39 people.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held employing children in hazardous manufacturing processes itself unconstitutional under Article 24, directed a Child Labour Rehabilitation-cum-Welfare Fund funded by employer fines, and linked the ruling to the DPSPs on child welfare. Central case for Article 24's prohibition on child employment in hazardous work." },
    { name:"People's Union for Democratic Rights v. Union of India (the 'Asiad Workers case')", cite:"AIR 1982 SC 1473; (1982) 3 SCC 235",
      facts:"Ahead of the 1982 Asian Games in Delhi, thousands of workers — recruited by contractors from poor villages across several states — were employed on stadium and infrastructure construction, paid below the statutory minimum wage, denied basic facilities, and effectively kept in bonded-labour-like conditions. Children under 14 were also employed in hazardous construction work. A letter from the People's Union for Democratic Rights documenting these conditions was treated by Justice Bhagwati as a writ petition.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held that paying workers less than the statutory minimum wage itself amounts to 'forced labour' prohibited under Article 23 — since a worker forced by economic desperation to accept sub-minimum wages has no genuine 'choice' in any meaningful sense. The Court also found clear violations of Article 24 (child labour in hazardous construction work) and read Article 21's right to life as including the right to live with basic human dignity. Significantly broadened Article 23's reach beyond obvious forms of bonded servitude to any exploitative underpayment, and further developed the relaxed locus standi rules enabling PIL." },
    { name:"Bandhua Mukti Morcha v. Union of India", cite:"AIR 1984 SC 802",
      facts:"An NGO dedicated to freeing bonded labourers surveyed stone quarries in Faridabad, Haryana, and found workers — migrants from several states — trapped in debt bondage, working in inhuman conditions with no freedom of movement, in violation of the Bonded Labour System (Abolition) Act, 1976. A letter documenting this was treated as a writ petition under Article 32.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held these conditions violated Articles 21 and 23, and — significantly — shifted the burden of proof onto the employer to disprove bonded labour once a worker showed signs of it, since proving bondage from the worker's side is often practically impossible given their vulnerability. Directed the Haryana government to identify, release and rehabilitate the bonded labourers. Extends PUDR's broad reading of Article 23 with a crucial evidentiary innovation (reversed burden of proof) making the constitutional prohibition on forced/bonded labour actually enforceable in practice, not just in principle." }
  ],
  "s3-const:u4:2": [
    { name:"L. Chandra Kumar v. Union of India", cite:"AIR 1997 SC 1125; (1997) 3 SCC 261",
      facts:"A law excluded High Court jurisdiction under Articles 226/227 over service matters, routing them exclusively through specialised Tribunals, with only the Supreme Court retaining oversight.",
      holding:"A 7-judge bench held judicial review under Articles 226/227 (High Courts) and 32 (Supreme Court) is part of the basic structure and cannot be excluded — Tribunals can decide first, but their orders remain subject to a High Court Division Bench. Central case on judicial review's constitutional status and the limits of ousting it." },
    { name:"Kesavananda Bharati v. State of Kerala", cite:"AIR 1973 SC 1461",
      facts:"The head of a religious mutt in Kerala challenged land reform legislation restricting the mutt's property rights, in a case that became the vehicle for a much larger question: does Parliament's power to amend the Constitution under Article 368 extend to abolishing judicial review and other core features entirely, or are there implicit limits on that power?",
      holding:"A 13-judge bench held Parliament can amend any part of the Constitution, including fundamental rights, but cannot alter its 'basic structure' — and judicial review was itself named among the basic features that cannot be removed by amendment, since without it there would be no mechanism to enforce any of the Constitution's other guarantees. The origin of the basic structure doctrine, and the case that first entrenched judicial review as constitutionally unamendable, later reaffirmed in Minerva Mills and L. Chandra Kumar." },
    { name:"Minerva Mills Ltd. v. Union of India", cite:"AIR 1980 SC 1789; (1980) 3 SCC 625",
      facts:"The 42nd Constitutional Amendment (passed during the Emergency) barred courts from reviewing constitutional amendments at all, and gave Directive Principles absolute primacy over Fundamental Rights. Minerva Mills, taken over under a law claimed to implement DPSPs, challenged this scheme.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court struck down the provision ousting judicial review of constitutional amendments, holding that judicial review is itself part of the basic structure and cannot be excluded even by amending the Constitution's amending power. Confirms, three years after L. Chandra Kumar's context and seven years after Kesavananda, that no branch of government — not even Parliament acting through Article 368 — can switch off the judiciary's power to review constitutional amendments for basic-structure compliance." }
  ],
  "s3-const:u4:3": [
    { name:"Vishaka & Ors. v. State of Rajasthan", cite:"AIR 1997 SC 3011; (1997) 6 SCC 241",
      facts:"Following the gang-rape of a social worker, Bhanwari Devi, and the total absence of any law on workplace sexual harassment, women's rights groups filed a PIL under Article 32.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court laid down binding workplace-harassment guidelines under Article 141, reading international conventions into the Constitution's equality guarantee in the total absence of legislation. A textbook example of PIL being used to fill a legislative gap and create enforceable rights." },
    { name:"S.P. Gupta v. Union of India (the 'First Judges Case')", cite:"AIR 1982 SC 149",
      facts:"A group of senior advocates, led by S.P. Gupta, challenged a Central Government circular on the short-term appointment and transfer of High Court judges without their consent, arguing it undermined judicial independence. The government challenged the advocates' standing (locus standi) to bring the case at all, since they were not personally, directly affected by the transfers.",
      holding:"Justice Bhagwati significantly relaxed the traditional rule of locus standi, holding that any public-spirited person or organisation acting in good faith can approach the court on behalf of a person or class of persons who, due to poverty, disability, or social/economic disadvantage, cannot approach the court themselves — even informal letters could be treated as writ petitions. This is the foundational case credited with formally establishing Public Interest Litigation as a distinct tool in Indian constitutional law, even though the Court's specific ruling on judicial appointments (favouring executive primacy) was itself later overturned in the Second Judges Case (1993)." },
    { name:"Hussainara Khatoon & Ors. v. Home Secretary, State of Bihar", cite:"1979 AIR 1369; 1979 SCR (3) 532",
      facts:"Newspaper reports and a habeas corpus petition brought to light that thousands of undertrial prisoners in Bihar's jails — many poor, illiterate, and without legal representation — had already spent more time in custody awaiting trial than the maximum sentence they could ever have received if convicted.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held the right to a speedy trial is part of the Article 21 right to life and personal liberty, and that free legal aid for the poor is a fundamental right, not mere state charity, since a fair trial is meaningless without access to a lawyer. Ordered the release of numerous prisoners and systemic reforms to the bail and legal-aid systems. One of the earliest and most consequential PILs after S.P. Gupta, showing how the relaxed standing rules could be immediately put to use to vindicate the rights of the poorest and most voiceless litigants." }
  ],
  "s3-const:u4:4": [
    { name:"L. Chandra Kumar v. Union of India", cite:"AIR 1997 SC 1125; (1997) 3 SCC 261",
      facts:"A law excluded High Court jurisdiction under Articles 226/227 over service matters, routing them exclusively through specialised Tribunals.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held that Article 226 writ jurisdiction of High Courts (broader than the Supreme Court's Article 32, since it covers 'any other purpose' beyond fundamental rights) is part of the basic structure and cannot be excluded by ordinary legislation. Central case for Article 226's scope and constitutional protection." },
    { name:"T.C. Basappa v. T. Nagappa", cite:"AIR 1954 SC 440",
      facts:"An Election Tribunal set aside T.C. Basappa's declared victory in a Mysore Legislative Assembly election, ruling in favour of the runner-up, T. Nagappa, on grounds Basappa argued were beyond the Tribunal's jurisdiction (including an improperly allowed amendment to the election petition and a disputed limitation period). Nagappa sought a writ of certiorari from the High Court to quash the original Tribunal decision, which the High Court granted; Basappa appealed to the Supreme Court.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court, tracing the writ of certiorari's origins to the English Crown's prerogative power of superintendence over inferior tribunals, held that a High Court's Article 226 certiorari jurisdiction allows it to quash decisions made without jurisdiction, in excess of jurisdiction, or containing an error of law apparent on the face of the record — but the High Court cannot act as an appellate body simply re-examining whether the decision was 'correct' on the merits. Foundational case explaining the precise scope of certiorari — one of the five prerogative writs available under Article 226 — and the crucial distinction between supervisory review (permitted) and appellate re-hearing (not permitted)." },
    { name:"A.K. Kraipak v. Union of India", cite:"AIR 1970 SC 150",
      facts:"A Selection Board choosing officers for the Indian Forest Service included the acting Chief Conservator of Forests, who was himself also a candidate for selection to the same service. Unsuccessful candidates challenged the selection, arguing the board member's dual role as both selector and candidate created a conflict of interest that tainted the entire process, even though the process was formally 'administrative' rather than a court-like proceeding.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held that natural justice principles — like the rule against bias (nemo judex in causa sua) — are not confined to judicial or quasi-judicial proceedings; they apply equally to purely administrative decisions that affect a person's rights or interests. A reasonable apprehension of bias, not proof of actual bias, is enough to vitiate a decision. Extends the scope of what a High Court can review under Article 226 — since 'administrative' action is no longer exempt from natural justice scrutiny, Article 226's supervisory reach effectively expanded to cover a much wider range of government decision-making beyond formal judicial or quasi-judicial bodies." }
  ],
  "s3-contract:u1:4": [
    { name:"Carlill v. Carbolic Smoke Ball Company", cite:"[1893] 1 QB 256 (Court of Appeal)",
      facts:"The Carbolic Smoke Ball Company advertised that its product would prevent influenza, promising £100 to anyone who used the smoke ball as directed and still caught flu, and stated it had deposited £1,000 in a bank 'to show sincerity'. Mrs Carlill bought and used the smoke ball as instructed but still caught influenza, and claimed the £100.",
      holding:"The Court of Appeal held the advertisement was a valid unilateral offer to the whole world (not a mere 'invitation to treat'), capable of being accepted by anyone who performed the stated conditions — no formal communication of acceptance was needed for a unilateral offer, since performance itself constitutes both acceptance and consideration. The bank deposit showed genuine intention to be legally bound, not just an advertising puff. The foundational case distinguishing a genuine unilateral offer from mere advertising talk." },
    { name:"Hyde v. Wrench", cite:"(1840) 3 Beav 334",
      facts:"Wrench offered to sell his farm to Hyde for £1,000. Hyde responded with a counter-offer of £950, which Wrench rejected. Hyde then tried to accept the original £1,000 offer, but Wrench refused to sell.",
      holding:"The Court held that a counter-offer destroys the original offer — Hyde's £950 counter-offer terminated Wrench's original £1,000 offer entirely, so there was nothing left for Hyde to later 'accept'. Foundational case establishing that a counter-offer is a rejection of the original offer, not merely a negotiating step that keeps it alive." },
    { name:"Felthouse v. Bindley", cite:"(1862) 11 CB (NS) 869",
      facts:"An uncle wrote to his nephew offering to buy a horse, adding 'if I hear no more about him, I consider the horse mine.' The nephew intended to accept but never communicated this back to his uncle; he simply told the auctioneer (Bindley) not to sell the horse, but Bindley mistakenly sold it anyway.",
      holding:"The Court held there was no valid contract — silence cannot constitute acceptance, even if the offeree privately intended to accept, unless acceptance is actually communicated to the offeror (or the offeror validly waives communication in a way the offeree acts on). Foundational case for the rule that acceptance must be communicated; an offeror cannot impose a contract by stipulating that silence will be treated as acceptance." },
    { name:"Lalman Shukla v. Gauri Dutt", cite:"(1913) 11 ALJ 489 (Allahabad High Court)",
      facts:"Gauri Dutt's nephew went missing. He sent his employee, Lalman Shukla, to search for the boy, without mentioning any reward. While Lalman was out searching, Gauri Dutt separately announced a reward for information leading to the boy's recovery. Lalman found the boy, unaware the reward had been announced, and later sued for the reward money.",
      holding:"The Allahabad High Court held that a person cannot accept an offer they were unaware of — since Lalman did not know about the reward when he found the boy, there was no valid acceptance and no contract for the reward. The leading Indian case establishing that acceptance requires knowledge of the offer at the time of the act relied on as acceptance." }
  ],
  "s3-contract:u2:2": [
    { name:"Mohori Bibee v. Dharmodas Ghose", cite:"ILR (1903) 30 Cal 539 (PC); (1903) 30 IA 114",
      facts:"Dharmodas Ghose, a minor, mortgaged his property to secure a loan of Rs. 20,000 from moneylender Brahmo Dutt, whose agent Kedar Nath knew Dharmodas was a minor at the time. Dharmodas later sued through his mother to have the mortgage declared void on grounds of his minority; the moneylender's estate argued Dharmodas should still be estopped from denying his contractual capacity, or at least required to repay the loan.",
      holding:"The Privy Council held that under Section 11 of the Indian Contract Act, a minor lacks capacity to contract, and any agreement with a minor is void ab initio — not merely voidable — from the very beginning. Since there was never a valid contract, the doctrine of estoppel could not apply (it presupposes a valid contract), and Sections 64/65 (restitution provisions) did not apply either, since they too presume a contract existed. The foundational Indian case on minors' contractual incapacity, holding minors cannot be compelled to repay benefits received under a void agreement." },
    { name:"Nash v. Inman", cite:"[1908] 2 KB 1",
      facts:"A Savile Row tailor, Nash, supplied a Cambridge undergraduate, Inman, with clothing including 11 fancy waistcoats, worth about £145, while Inman was still a minor. Inman refused to pay, and Nash sued for the price, arguing the clothing qualified as 'necessaries' — an exception under which a minor can still be bound to pay a reasonable price.",
      holding:"The Court of Appeal held Nash could not recover, since it was shown Inman already had an adequate supply of clothing suitable to his station in life — 'necessaries' must be goods the minor actually needed at the time, not merely goods generally appropriate to their social class. The burden is on the person seeking payment to prove both suitability and actual need. The essential complementary case to Mohori Bibee: while a minor's ordinary contract is void, this establishes the narrow 'necessaries' exception (mirrored in Section 68 of the Indian Contract Act) and how strictly courts apply it." },
    { name:"R. Leslie Ltd. v. Sheill", cite:"[1914] 3 KB 607",
      facts:"Sheill, a minor, fraudulently told moneylenders R. Leslie Ltd. that he was of full age in order to obtain two loans totalling £400. He spent the money and, when he failed to repay, the lenders sued — not to enforce the loan contract itself (which was void due to his minority), but for the return of the money on the basis of his fraud, framing it as a tort/equitable restitution claim rather than a contract claim.",
      holding:"The Court of Appeal held Sheill was not liable, since the money had already been spent and could not be traced into any specific remaining asset — ordering him to repay an equivalent sum would, in substance, be enforcing the very void contract the law refuses to enforce, merely relabelled as 'restitution'. Equitable restitution against a minor is only available where identifiable property obtained by the fraud still exists in their hands. Shows the outer limit of a minor's protection even against their own fraud, and complements Mohori Bibee by confirming Indian and English law take the same strict line: a minor's fraud does not open a back door to enforcing what contract law itself declares void." }
  ],
  "s3-contract:u2:4": [
    { name:"Raffles v. Wichelhaus (the 'Peerless' case)", cite:"(1864) 2 H&C 906",
      facts:"A contract was made for the sale of cotton to arrive 'ex Peerless from Bombay'. Unknown to either party, there were two different ships named Peerless sailing from Bombay at different times — the buyer meant the ship sailing in October, the seller meant the one sailing in December. When the seller tried to deliver cotton from the December ship, the buyer refused, claiming he had contracted for the October shipment.",
      holding:"The Court held there was no valid contract at all — since each party reasonably but mistakenly believed the other meant a different ship, there was no genuine 'meeting of the minds' (consensus ad idem) on an essential term. This is the foundational case for mutual/bilateral mistake as to a fundamental term of the contract, rendering the agreement void for lack of true agreement." },
    { name:"Chikkam Ammiraju v. Chikkam Seshamma", cite:"(1917) 32 MLJ 494; 34 Ind Cas 578 (Madras High Court)",
      facts:"Swami threatened to commit suicide unless his wife and son executed a release deed relinquishing their property rights in his favour. Under this pressure, they executed the deed. When Swami's brothers later sought to enforce the deed, the wife and son argued it was obtained through coercion and should be set aside.",
      holding:"A majority of the Madras High Court held that a threat of suicide amounts to 'coercion' under Section 15 of the Indian Contract Act — since abetment of suicide is an act forbidden by the Penal Code, even though the completed act of suicide itself cannot be punished (a dead person cannot be punished). This significantly broadened 'coercion' beyond physical force or unlawful detention of property to include serious psychological/emotional pressure, and confirmed that coercion invalidating consent need not come from a party to the contract itself." },
    { name:"Cundy v. Lindsay", cite:"(1878) 3 App Cas 459",
      facts:"A rogue, Alfred Blenkarn, wrote to linen manufacturers Lindsay & Co. from an address on Wood Street, deliberately signing his name to closely resemble a reputable firm, Blenkiron & Co., located nearby on the same street. Believing they were dealing with the reputable firm, Lindsay & Co. sent a large order of handkerchiefs on credit. Blenkarn then sold the handkerchiefs on to an innocent third party, Cundy, and disappeared without paying Lindsay & Co.",
      holding:"The House of Lords held there was no contract at all between Lindsay & Co. and Blenkarn — Lindsay & Co. intended to deal only with Blenkiron & Co., never with Blenkarn, so there was no genuine consensus ad idem (meeting of minds) as to who the other contracting party even was. Since no contract existed, no title in the goods ever passed to Blenkarn, meaning he had nothing to sell — so Cundy, despite being an entirely innocent purchaser, had to return the goods. The classic case for mistake as to identity (distinct from Raffles' mistake as to subject-matter) rendering a contract void rather than merely voidable, illustrating how harshly the law can fall on an innocent third party caught between two other parties' dealings." }
  ],
  "s3-contract:u2:5": [
    { name:"Bisset v. Wilkinson", cite:"[1927] AC 177 (Privy Council, New Zealand)",
      facts:"A seller of farmland told the buyer the land could carry approximately 2,000 sheep, though the seller had never actually used the land for sheep farming himself and made clear this was his estimate. The land could not in fact support that many sheep, and the disappointed buyer sued for misrepresentation.",
      holding:"The Privy Council held this was a statement of honest opinion, not a statement of fact, since both parties knew the seller had no special sheep-farming experience with this specific land — an honestly held opinion that turns out to be wrong is not actionable misrepresentation, unlike a false statement of fact. Key case for distinguishing actionable factual misrepresentation from non-actionable opinion, especially where the speaker's limited expertise is apparent to both sides." },
    { name:"Derry v. Peek", cite:"(1889) 14 App Cas 337",
      facts:"A tramway company's prospectus stated it had the right to use steam power instead of horses to run its trams, when in fact this right still required approval from the Board of Trade, which the directors genuinely (though it turned out wrongly) believed was a mere formality. When approval was refused and the company collapsed, shareholder Sir Henry Peek — who had bought shares relying on the prospectus — sued the directors for fraudulent misrepresentation (the tort of deceit).",
      holding:"The House of Lords held the directors were NOT liable for fraud, since fraud requires a false statement made knowingly, without belief in its truth, or recklessly careless as to its truth — an honest (even if unreasonable) belief in the statement's truth defeats a fraud claim, though it may still ground a civil claim for negligent misrepresentation instead. This case established the strict three-part test for the tort of deceit/fraudulent misrepresentation, distinguishing it from the lower threshold needed for civil (non-fraudulent) misrepresentation or negligence." },
    { name:"Smith v. Land and House Property Corporation", cite:"(1884) LR 28 Ch D 7",
      facts:"A hotel seller advertised that the property was let to a tenant, Fleck, describing him as 'a most desirable tenant' — when in fact the seller knew Fleck was significantly behind on rent and in serious financial trouble; Fleck went bankrupt shortly before the sale completed. The buyer refused to complete, and the seller sued for specific performance, arguing the description was merely an opinion, not a statement of fact.",
      holding:"The Court of Appeal held the statement was an actionable misrepresentation, not mere opinion — where facts are not equally known to both parties, a statement of opinion by the person who knows the facts best implicitly asserts that facts exist to justify that opinion. Since the seller knew facts that plainly contradicted 'most desirable', the opinion was itself a misleading statement of fact. The essential contrast case to Bisset v. Wilkinson: an opinion is only safely non-actionable where both sides have genuinely equal access to the underlying facts — where the speaker has superior knowledge, their 'opinion' can itself be a misrepresentation." }
  ],
  "s3-contract:u4:2": [
    { name:"Cutter v. Powell", cite:"(1795) 6 TR 320",
      facts:"Cutter was hired as second mate on a ship for a voyage from Jamaica to Liverpool, to be paid a lump sum only 'on his arrival' at Liverpool. Cutter died partway through the voyage, having performed most of it. His widow sued for a proportionate share of the wages for the part of the voyage he completed.",
      holding:"The Court held Cutter's widow could recover nothing — the contract was for entire, indivisible performance (completing the whole voyage) as a condition precedent to any payment, and partial performance (however substantial) does not entitle a party to proportionate payment on such an 'entire contract'. Foundational case establishing the harsh 'entire contract' rule, later softened by the doctrine of substantial performance and quantum meruit in appropriate cases." },
    { name:"Sumpter v. Hedges", cite:"[1898] 1 QB 673",
      facts:"A builder agreed to construct buildings for a fixed lump sum but abandoned the work after completing about two-thirds of it, owing to lack of funds. The landowner then finished the buildings himself using some of the builder's materials left on site. The builder sued for the value of work done.",
      holding:"The Court of Appeal held the builder could not recover payment for the incomplete work under the original contract, since it was an entire contract requiring full completion — but could recover the reasonable value of materials the landowner chose to use, on a separate quasi-contractual basis. Illustrates that voluntarily accepting the benefit of leftover materials is different from voluntarily accepting incomplete performance of the main obligation, which does not by itself imply a fresh agreement to pay a reasonable sum." },
    { name:"Bolton v. Mahadeva", cite:"[1972] 1 WLR 1009 (Court of Appeal)",
      facts:"A contractor was engaged to install a central heating system in the defendant's house for a lump sum. The completed system was so defective — giving off fumes and heating inadequately — that it would have cost roughly a third of the contract price to fix. The homeowner refused to pay anything, and the contractor sued for the contract price.",
      holding:"The Court of Appeal held the contractor could recover nothing, since the defects were so substantial that the contract had not been substantially performed — the doctrine of substantial performance (allowing recovery minus the cost of defects) only applies where performance is essentially complete with minor defects, not where the defects go to the very core of what was promised. Illustrates the boundary of 'substantial performance' as an exception to the strict entire-contract rule in Cutter v. Powell." },
    { name:"Ritchie v. Atkinson", cite:"(1808) 10 East 295",
      facts:"A ship's master contracted to carry a full cargo of hemp and iron for an agreed freight rate per ton, to be paid on completion of the voyage. The master delivered only part of the contracted cargo, having failed to load the rest.",
      holding:"The Court held that where a contract is severable/divisible into distinct parts (here, freight was payable per ton actually carried, not as one indivisible lump sum for the whole cargo), a party who performs part of it can recover a proportionate payment for the part actually performed, even though the whole contract was not fulfilled. Contrasted with the 'entire contract' approach in Cutter v. Powell, illustrating how a contract's own structure (divisible vs entire) determines whether partial performance earns partial payment." }
  ],
  "s3-contract:u3:4": [
    { name:"Raffles v. Wichelhaus (the 'Peerless' case)", cite:"(1864) 2 H&C 906",
      facts:"A contract was made for cotton to arrive 'ex Peerless from Bombay'. Unknown to either side, two ships named Peerless sailed from Bombay at different times — buyer and seller each meant a different one.",
      holding:"The Court held there was no valid contract, since there was no genuine meeting of the minds on an essential term. Foundational case for mutual mistake as to a fundamental term, relevant here to how mistake affects performance and discharge, not just formation." },
    { name:"Chikkam Ammiraju v. Chikkam Seshamma", cite:"(1917) 32 MLJ 494; 34 Ind Cas 578 (Madras High Court)",
      facts:"Swami threatened suicide unless his wife and son executed a release deed relinquishing property rights, and they did so under that pressure.",
      holding:"The Madras High Court held a threat of suicide constitutes 'coercion' under Section 15, broadening coercion beyond physical force to include serious psychological pressure — relevant here to how vitiated free consent affects a contract's validity and dischargeability." },
    { name:"Allcard v. Skinner", cite:"(1887) 36 Ch D 145",
      facts:"Miss Allcard, introduced to a religious sisterhood by her spiritual advisor, joined the order and — under its rules requiring vows of poverty and obedience, and forbidding members from seeking outside advice — gave away her entire fortune in cash and shares to the sisterhood's lady superior, Miss Skinner. Years after leaving the order, Allcard sought to reclaim the property, arguing the gifts were the product of undue influence arising from her total spiritual submission to Skinner's authority.",
      holding:"The Court of Appeal held the relationship between Allcard and Skinner did raise a presumption of undue influence, given the deep spiritual authority and the rule against seeking independent advice — but ultimately barred her claim on the separate ground of laches (unreasonable delay), since she waited six years after leaving before asserting her rights. The leading case establishing that undue influence can be presumed from certain relationships of trust and dependence (not just proven through direct evidence of pressure), distinct from coercion (which involves threats) and from misrepresentation/fraud (which involves false statements) — completing the full picture of vitiating factors alongside Raffles (mistake) and Chikkam Ammiraju (coercion)." }
  ],
  "s3-family:u2:1": [
    { name:"Smt. Seema v. Ashwani Kumar", cite:"(2006) 2 SCC 578",
      facts:"During a bitter matrimonial dispute transferred to the Supreme Court, it emerged that the absence of any uniform, compulsory system for registering marriages across Indian states was letting many husbands simply deny a marriage had ever taken place — a problem disproportionately harming wives seeking to prove their marital status for maintenance, inheritance, or custody claims. The National Commission for Women supported the case for compulsory registration.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court directed every State and Union Territory to notify rules making registration of marriages compulsory, regardless of the religion of the parties, holding this was necessary in the public interest to curb child marriages, prevent bigamy, and protect women's rights. The Court clarified that registration is procedural evidence of a marriage, not a precondition for its validity under personal law — an unregistered marriage solemnised per proper ceremonies remains legally valid, but registration provides crucial proof." },
    { name:"Bhaurao Shankar Lokhande & Anr. v. State of Maharashtra", cite:"AIR 1965 SC 1564",
      facts:"Bhaurao Lokhande, already validly married under proper Hindu ceremonies, went through a second marriage ceremony while his first wife was still alive, and was prosecuted for bigamy — but the prosecution could not prove the essential ceremonies (like saptapadi) had actually been performed for the second marriage.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held that a marriage is only validly 'solemnized' if its essential ceremonies were actually performed — a couple cannot be treated as legally married, for bigamy or any other purpose, merely because they went through some form of ceremony or considered themselves married. Directly illustrates why registration matters as evidentiary proof: without documentation, even establishing that a marriage's essential ceremonies occurred becomes a serious evidentiary burden, exactly the gap Seema v. Ashwani Kumar's compulsory registration was designed to close." },
    { name:"Lata Singh v. State of U.P. & Anr.", cite:"(2006) 5 SCC 475; AIR 2006 SC 2522",
      facts:"Lata Singh, a 27-year-old graduate, married Brahma Nand Gupta — a man from a different caste — of her own free will at an Arya Samaj temple. Her brothers, opposed to the inter-caste marriage, filed a false police report claiming she had been kidnapped, leading to the arrest of her husband's relatives, and continued threatening and harassing the couple even after Lata formally confirmed to a magistrate that she had married voluntarily as a consenting adult.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court quashed the criminal proceedings, holding that an adult has an absolute right under Article 21 to marry a person of their choice regardless of caste or family objection, and directed police across the country to take immediate protective action against threats or violence targeting inter-caste or inter-religious couples. Though centred on the right to marry rather than registration specifically, it reinforces why documented, registrable marriages matter — a properly evidenced marriage is a couple's strongest shield against exactly this kind of family-driven false criminal harassment, giving Seema v. Ashwani Kumar's registration mandate real practical teeth." }
  ],
  "s3-family:u3:1": [
    { name:"Naveen Kohli v. Neelu Kohli", cite:"(2006) 4 SCC 558; AIR 2006 SC 1675",
      facts:"Naveen and Neelu Kohli's marriage deteriorated over years into a pattern of mutual hostility — repeated criminal complaints filed against each other, allegations of infidelity, and a prolonged separation with no possibility of reconciliation. Naveen sought divorce under Section 13 of the Hindu Marriage Act primarily on the ground of cruelty.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court granted divorce, holding that Neelu's conduct — repeated litigation, false accusations, and sustained hostility — amounted to mental cruelty. Beyond deciding the case, the Court urged Parliament to formally add 'irretrievable breakdown of marriage' as a distinct statutory ground for divorce under the Hindu Marriage Act, since forcing parties to remain legally bound to a marriage that has completely and permanently broken down serves no purpose. Though Parliament has not yet acted on this recommendation, courts (and the Supreme Court itself, using Article 142) have since relied on this reasoning to grant divorces on facts amounting to irretrievable breakdown." },
    { name:"Samar Ghosh v. Jaya Ghosh", cite:"(2007) 4 SCC 511",
      facts:"Two senior IAS officers, married since 1984, saw their relationship deteriorate — the wife's refusal to have children, prolonged periods of living separately within the same house, and her described indifference during the husband's illness. He sought divorce on the ground of cruelty; the trial court granted it, but the High Court reversed, finding the evidence insufficient to establish cruelty.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court restored the divorce decree and, more significantly, laid down a detailed (though expressly non-exhaustive) list of illustrative examples of what can constitute 'mental cruelty' under Section 13(1)(i-a) — including sustained, unjustifiable conduct causing mental pain, studied neglect, and total indifference to the other spouse's feelings, assessed cumulatively rather than through isolated incidents. Clarified that ordinary wear-and-tear of married life, minor irritations, and normal disagreements do not amount to cruelty. The most detailed and frequently cited framework for assessing mental cruelty claims in Indian matrimonial litigation, complementing Naveen Kohli's broader push for recognising irretrievable breakdown." },
    { name:"V. Bhagat v. D. Bhagat", cite:"(1994) 1 SCC 337",
      facts:"A husband, a practising advocate, sought divorce alleging his wife's adultery. She responded in her formal written pleadings by describing him and his entire family as suffering from 'mental hallucination', 'paranoid disorder', and being 'lunatics' — allegations she never withdrew even after the husband amended his petition to instead seek divorce on the ground of mental cruelty caused by these very statements. The litigation had dragged on for over eight years.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court granted divorce on the ground of mental cruelty, holding that making false, defamatory, and deeply humiliating allegations against a spouse and their family in formal court pleadings — allegations of insanity, no less — itself constitutes cruelty of a kind that makes it impossible to reasonably expect the parties to live together again, regardless of how the litigation started. Also exercised its extraordinary power under Article 142 to grant the divorce directly rather than remit the case for further years of trial, given the marriage was self-evidently and irretrievably broken. Illustrates that the litigation process itself — not just pre-litigation conduct — can generate the cruelty that justifies divorce." }
  ],
  "s3-family:u3:2": [
    { name:"Shayara Bano v. Union of India", cite:"(2017) 9 SCC 1; AIR 2017 SC 4609",
      facts:"After 15 years of marriage, Rizwan Ahmad divorced his wife Shayara Bano by pronouncing 'talaq' three times in one sitting (talaq-e-biddat/instant triple talaq) — an irrevocable divorce taking effect immediately, without any attempt at reconciliation, notice, or opportunity for her to be heard. Bano filed a writ petition challenging the constitutional validity of this practice.",
      holding:"A 5-judge bench, by 3:2 majority, held talaq-e-biddat unconstitutional — manifestly arbitrary and violative of Article 14, since it allows a marital tie to be broken 'capriciously and whimsically' with no chance to save it. The majority held it was not protected as an essential religious practice under Article 25. Landmark case ending the legal validity of instant triple talaq in India, later followed by the Muslim Women (Protection of Rights on Marriage) Act, 2019, criminalising the practice." },
    { name:"Shamim Ara v. State of U.P.", cite:"(2002) 7 SCC 518",
      facts:"Shamim Ara sought maintenance for herself and her children from her husband, Abrar Ahmad, who claimed in his written statement — filed in the maintenance proceedings themselves — that he had already divorced her by triple talaq years earlier, and so owed her nothing. He offered no independent proof of when, how, or in whose presence the talaq was actually pronounced.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held that merely asserting a past divorce in court pleadings, without proof of an actual pronouncement, cannot itself count as a valid talaq — a valid talaq requires proof it was genuinely pronounced, for a reasonable cause, and preceded by an attempt at reconciliation, per the Quranic requirements as explained in earlier Islamic jurisprudence (Danial Latifi's reasoning and classical commentators). Since no valid talaq was proven, the marriage subsisted and the maintenance obligation continued. A crucial pre-Shayara Bano case establishing that even a form of talaq theoretically permitted still requires proper procedural validity — you cannot retroactively manufacture a divorce simply by claiming one occurred." },
    { name:"Jiauddin Ahmed v. Anwara Begum", cite:"(1981) 1 GLR 358 (Gauhati High Court)",
      facts:"Anwara Begum sought maintenance from her husband, Jiauddin Ahmed, under Section 125 CrPC. He claimed he had already divorced her by pronouncing talaq, registered at the local Kazi's office, and so owed her nothing beyond the iddat period. No evidence was produced of any attempt at reconciliation before the talaq, nor of the exact circumstances of its pronouncement.",
      holding:"Justice Baharul Islam (later a Supreme Court judge) held that the correct position under the Quran is that talaq must be for a reasonable cause, and must be preceded by attempts at reconciliation between the spouses through two arbiters — one nominated by each family; if these attempts fail, the talaq may then be effected. This decades-earlier Gauhati High Court ruling — decided together with the companion case Rukia Khatun v. Abdul Khalique Laskar — was expressly approved and relied upon by the Supreme Court itself in Shamim Ara, making it the true doctrinal foundation for the modern, procedurally-strict understanding of valid talaq that both Shamim Ara and eventually Shayara Bano built upon." }
  ],
  "s3-family:u3:3": [
    { name:"Mohd. Ahmed Khan v. Shah Bano Begum", cite:"AIR 1985 SC 945; (1985) 2 SCC 556",
      facts:"After over 40 years of marriage, Mohd. Ahmed Khan divorced his 62-year-old wife, Shah Bano, by irrevocable talaq. She sought maintenance under Section 125 of the CrPC, a secular provision requiring anyone with sufficient means to maintain a wife unable to maintain herself. Her husband argued his obligation ended after the Islamic 'iddat' period (roughly three months post-divorce), per Muslim personal law.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held Section 125 CrPC applies to all citizens regardless of religion, and a Muslim husband's duty to maintain a divorced wife unable to support herself extends beyond the iddat period if she has no other means of support. The judgment sparked major political controversy, leading Parliament to pass the Muslim Women (Protection of Rights on Divorce) Act, 1986 — later interpreted in Danial Latifi v. Union of India (2001) to still require adequate provision extending effectively beyond iddat, preserving the substance of the Shah Bano ruling within the new statute." },
    { name:"Danial Latifi v. Union of India", cite:"AIR 2001 SC 3958; (2001) 7 SCC 740",
      facts:"After the political backlash to Shah Bano, Parliament passed the Muslim Women (Protection of Rights on Divorce) Act, 1986, whose text appeared to limit a husband's maintenance duty to the iddat period alone. The Act's constitutionality was challenged as discriminating against Muslim women relative to the broader protection under the secular Section 125 CrPC.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court upheld the 1986 Act's constitutionality by interpreting it purposively: Section 3 requires the husband to make 'reasonable and fair provision' within the iddat period, but that provision must be calculated to sustain the wife for her entire remaining life if she doesn't remarry — not merely cover three months. Directly relevant to how the 1986 Act (the very subject of this topic) operates in practice: it formally limits the obligation's timing but not its actual adequacy, preserving Shah Bano's substance through statutory interpretation rather than reopening the constitutional fight." },
    { name:"Iqbal Bano v. State of U.P.", cite:"(2007) 6 SCC 785",
      facts:"Iqbal Bano, married in 1959, filed for maintenance under Section 125 CrPC after her husband stopped supporting her. He responded that he had already divorced her by pronouncing talaq thrice, that he had discharged his obligations under Muslim law by paying mahr, and that under the Muslim Women (Protection of Rights on Divorce) Act, 1986 she could no longer proceed under Section 125 CrPC at all, only under the 1986 Act's own separate procedure.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held that a Section 125 CrPC application filed by a Muslim woman can, where appropriate, be treated by the Magistrate as an application under Section 3 of the 1986 Act instead — the two are not mutually exclusive procedural tracks — and reaffirmed that liability under Section 3(1)(a) is not confined to the iddat period. Also reaffirmed Shamim Ara's requirement that talaq must be actually proven, not merely pleaded. Directly on point for interpreting how the 1986 Act (this topic's subject) and the general CrPC maintenance regime work together rather than as rigid alternatives." }
  ],
  "s3-family:u4:0": [
    { name:"Danial Latifi v. Union of India", cite:"AIR 2001 SC 3958; (2001) 7 SCC 740",
      facts:"After the Shah Bano controversy, Parliament passed the Muslim Women (Protection of Rights on Divorce) Act, 1986, which appeared to limit a Muslim husband's maintenance obligation to only the iddat period. The Act's constitutionality was challenged as discriminatory against Muslim women compared to the wider protection available to other women under Section 125 CrPC.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court upheld the 1986 Act's constitutionality, but interpreted it purposively — holding that Section 3 of the Act requires a husband to make 'reasonable and fair provision' for his divorced wife's future within the iddat period, but that provision must be sufficient to maintain her for her entire remaining life if she doesn't remarry, not just for three months. This reading preserved the practical substance of the Shah Bano ruling while formally operating within the 1986 Act's framework — essential case for understanding how Muslim women's maintenance rights actually work in India today." },
    { name:"Noor Saba Khatoon v. Mohd. Quasim", cite:"AIR 1997 SC 3280",
      facts:"After Mohd. Quasim divorced his wife, Noor Saba Khatoon, and remarried, a trial court ordered him to pay maintenance to both her and their three minor children. He sought to limit his liability, arguing the Muslim Women (Protection of Rights on Divorce) Act, 1986 confined his maintenance obligations — including toward the children — to the iddat period alone, just as it did for the wife.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held that the 1986 Act's limitations apply only to the divorced wife's own maintenance — it does not touch a Muslim father's separate, ongoing obligation to maintain his minor children under Section 125 CrPC, which continues until they reach majority (or, for daughters, until marriage) regardless of the parents' divorce. Crucial clarification that the 1986 Act was never intended to cut off children's independent maintenance rights — it only reshaped the wife's claim, leaving Section 125's protection for children of divorced Muslim parents fully intact." },
    { name:"Shabana Bano v. Imran Khan", cite:"(2010) 1 SCC 666; decided 4 December 2009",
      facts:"Shabana Bano, married under Muslim rites, filed for maintenance under Section 125 CrPC after her husband failed to support her following her pregnancy. He claimed to have divorced her, and argued that under the Muslim Women (Protection of Rights on Divorce) Act, 1986, his liability ended at the iddat period, and that a Family Court could not even entertain a Section 125 CrPC petition from a divorced Muslim woman at all.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held that a divorced Muslim woman remains entitled to invoke Section 125 CrPC even after the 1986 Act's enactment — the Act supplements, and does not replace or exclude, her right to seek maintenance under the general secular provision, and this right continues as long as she has not remarried, regardless of the iddat period's expiry. Directly resolves the practical jurisdictional confusion this topic centres on: divorced Muslim women can choose either the 1986 Act's own procedure or Section 125 CrPC, reaffirming Danial Latifi's substantive protection with unambiguous procedural clarity." }
  ],
  "s3-family:u4:2": [
    { name:"Githa Hariharan v. Reserve Bank of India", cite:"AIR 1999 SC 1149; (1999) 2 SCC 228",
      facts:"Githa Hariharan applied to the Reserve Bank of India for investment bonds in her minor son's name, signing as his guardian. The RBI rejected the application, insisting on either the father's signature or a certificate showing the father was unfit — relying on Section 6(a) of the Hindu Minority and Guardianship Act, 1956, which names the father as natural guardian and the mother as guardian only 'after' him.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court upheld Section 6(a)'s constitutional validity but reinterpreted the word 'after' to mean 'in the absence of' — covering not just the father's death, but also situations where he is unavailable, indifferent, or has relinquished his role in the child's affairs by mutual agreement or conduct — rather than reading it strictly as 'after the father's lifetime'. This reading avoided striking down the provision while removing its gender-discriminatory practical effect, allowing mothers to act as natural guardians even while the father is alive, where circumstances warrant it." },
    { name:"ABC v. The State (NCT of Delhi)", cite:"(2015) 10 SCC 1",
      facts:"An unwed, well-educated, financially independent Christian mother had raised her son alone since birth, with no involvement from the biological father. She petitioned to be appointed his sole guardian under the Guardians and Wards Act, 1890, but refused — citing privacy and the father's total absence from the child's life — to disclose his identity, which the Act's procedure normally requires so the father can be notified. The Delhi High Court dismissed her petition for this refusal.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court allowed her appeal, holding that the paramount consideration under Section 7 of the Act is the child's welfare, not rigid procedural compliance — since Hindu and Muslim law both already recognise the mother as an unwed child's natural custodian without notice to an absent father, denying a Christian mother the same practical relief created an unprincipled gap. Confirms that an unwed mother can be appointed sole guardian without disclosing or notifying the biological father, when doing so serves the child's genuine welfare — the natural extension of Githa Hariharan's welfare-centred approach to guardianship into the unwed-parent context." },
    { name:"Rosy Jacob v. Jacob A. Chakramakkal", cite:"1973 AIR 2090; (1973) 1 SCC 840",
      facts:"After a judicially separated couple's marriage broke down amid allegations of cruelty, custody of their three children was split — the eldest son with the father, the daughter and youngest son with the mother, who ran a school and could offer them an educational environment. Years later, the father sought custody of all three children under Section 25 of the Guardians and Wards Act, arguing his statutory position as natural guardian entitled him to it, especially now that circumstances had changed.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held that a father's statutory status as natural guardian does not give him an automatic or indefeasible right to custody — the child's welfare is always the paramount and controlling consideration, and a parent's personal fitness alone does not settle the question if the other parent can serve the child's welfare equally well or better. Famously observed that children 'are not mere chattels' or 'playthings for their parents'. The foundational case establishing welfare-as-paramount over parental statutory rights in Indian custody law, later extended by Githa Hariharan (removing gender bias in guardianship itself) and ABC v. State (extending welfare-centred reasoning to unwed parents)." }
  ],
  "s1-legal:u1:1": [
    { name:"Vishaka & Ors. v. State of Rajasthan", cite:"AIR 1997 SC 3011; (1997) 6 SCC 241",
      facts:"Bhanwari Devi, a social worker under Rajasthan's Women Development Programme, was gang-raped by influential men in her village in retaliation for trying to stop a child marriage. When the trial court acquitted the accused for lack of evidence, women's rights groups filed a PIL under Article 32, seeking enforcement of fundamental rights under Articles 14, 15, 19(1)(g) and 21 in the absence of any specific law on workplace sexual harassment.",
      holding:"A 3-judge bench (Verma CJI, Manohar and Kirpal JJ.) held that in the absence of domestic legislation, international conventions like CEDAW could be read into the guarantee of gender equality under the Constitution, and laid down binding guidelines under Article 141 requiring employers to prevent and redress workplace sexual harassment. A textbook illustration of law's 'social engineering' function — the Court effectively legislated a gap-filling framework later codified as the POSH Act, 2013." },
    { name:"M.C. Mehta v. Union of India (Oleum Gas Leak case)", cite:"(1987) 1 SCC 395; AIR 1987 SC 1086",
      facts:"Oleum gas leaked from Shriram Foods and Fertilizers' plant in a densely populated part of Delhi in December 1985, killing an advocate and injuring several others. M.C. Mehta had already filed a PIL under Articles 21 and 32 seeking closure or relocation of hazardous industries from residential areas, so the Constitution Bench used the pending petition to decide the standard of liability for such enterprises.",
      holding:"The Court, led by Bhagwati CJ, refused to be confined by the 19th-century English rule of strict liability in Rylands v. Fletcher and instead evolved an entirely new principle — 'absolute liability' — under which a hazardous enterprise is unconditionally liable for harm caused, with none of the traditional defenses (act of God, third-party act, plaintiff's own fault) available, and compensation must scale with the enterprise's size. A clear example of courts performing a genuinely legislative function by creating new law where none existed." },
    { name:"Olga Tellis & Ors. v. Bombay Municipal Corporation", cite:"1985 (3) SCC 545; AIR 1986 SC 180",
      facts:"Pavement and slum dwellers in Bombay, mostly migrants who had settled near their places of informal work, faced eviction without notice under Section 314 of the Bombay Municipal Corporation Act, 1888, as part of a city 'beautification' drive. Journalist Olga Tellis and others filed a PIL arguing that Section 314's no-notice eviction procedure violated Article 21, since losing their pavement dwelling would also cost them their livelihood.",
      holding:"A 5-judge bench (Chandrachud CJ) held that the right to life under Article 21 is broad enough to include the right to livelihood, since a person deprived of livelihood is as good as deprived of life itself — but ultimately still permitted eviction, provided it followed a fair procedure with notice and hearing. Shows law's 'social' function through purposive interpretation, expanding a bare constitutional text to reach lived socio-economic reality, even while balancing it against public order." }
  ],
  "s1-legal:u2:2": [
    { name:"Municipal Corporation of Delhi v. Gurnam Kaur", cite:"AIR 1989 SC 38; (1989) 1 SCC 101",
      facts:"The Delhi High Court had directed the Municipal Corporation to allot a pavement stall to Gurnam Kaur, relying on an earlier Supreme Court direction in the 'Jamna Das' case that had been made purely by consent of the parties, without argument or reasoning, and with an express reservation that it not be treated as precedent.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held that a decision not founded on reasons or arrived at without considering the issue cannot be a binding 'law declared' under Article 141 — pronouncements outside the ratio decidendi are mere obiter dicta with no binding force, and a decision given in ignorance of a binding statutory provision is per incuriam. The standard case for teaching the difference between ratio decidendi, obiter dicta, and how consent orders and per incuriam decisions fail to bind future courts." },
    { name:"Bengal Immunity Co. Ltd. v. State of Bihar", cite:"AIR 1955 SC 661",
      facts:"Bengal Immunity Company, a Calcutta-based manufacturer with no presence in Bihar, was sought to be taxed by Bihar on inter-state sales delivered there. To decide the case, a 7-judge bench had to first confront a preliminary question: whether the Supreme Court was bound by its own earlier ruling in State of Bombay v. United Motors (1953), which had interpreted Article 286 the opposite way.",
      holding:"The Court held that, unlike a subordinate court, the Supreme Court is not absolutely bound by its own previous decisions and may reconsider and overrule them — particularly on constitutional interpretation — if satisfied the earlier view was wrong, and went on to overrule United Motors. Establishes that the Indian Supreme Court follows a more flexible form of stare decisis than the traditional English rule of near-total self-binding precedent." },
    { name:"Central Board of Dawoodi Bohra Community v. State of Maharashtra", cite:"(2005) 2 SCC 673",
      facts:"A 2-judge bench doubted the correctness of a 1962 ruling by a 5-judge bench (Sardar Syedna Taher Saifuddin Saheb) on the constitutionality of a law restricting religious excommunication, and wanted to refer the matter to a larger bench. A 5-judge Constitution Bench was convened to first settle the procedural question of whether a smaller bench can do this on its own.",
      holding:"The Court held that a bench of lesser or co-equal strength cannot itself disagree with or dissent from a decision of a bench of larger strength — it can, at most, invite the Chief Justice's attention and request that the matter be placed before a bench of equal-or-greater strength than the one being doubted. Establishes that within India's precedent hierarchy, it is bench strength (quorum), not just chronology, that determines which decision binds which." }
  ],
  "s1-legal:u3:3": [
    { name:"L. Chandra Kumar v. Union of India", cite:"AIR 1997 SC 1125; (1997) 3 SCC 261",
      facts:"Section 28 of the Administrative Tribunals Act, 1985 (enacted under Article 323A) excluded the jurisdiction of High Courts under Articles 226/227 over service matters, routing them instead through specialised Tribunals, with only the Supreme Court retaining oversight under Article 136. A 7-judge bench was constituted to reconsider the earlier decision in Sampath Kumar (1987) that had upheld this scheme.",
      holding:"The Supreme Court held that the power of judicial review of High Courts under Articles 226/227 (and of the Supreme Court under Article 32) is part of the Constitution's basic structure and cannot be ousted by ordinary legislation — Tribunals can function as courts of first instance, but their orders remain subject to scrutiny by a Division Bench of the jurisdictional High Court. Central case for understanding the hierarchy of courts, judicial review, and how the basic structure doctrine constrains legislative restructuring of that hierarchy." },
    { name:"Minerva Mills Ltd. v. Union of India", cite:"AIR 1980 SC 1789; (1980) 3 SCC 625",
      facts:"The 42nd Constitutional Amendment, passed during the Emergency, inserted clauses (4) and (5) into Article 368, purporting to give Parliament unlimited power to amend the Constitution and to exclude any judicial review of such amendments. Minerva Mills, a nationalised textile company, challenged the amendment alongside a broader constitutional question about the relationship between Fundamental Rights and Directive Principles.",
      holding:"A 5-judge bench (Chandrachud CJ) struck down clauses (4) and (5) of Article 368 as unconstitutional, holding that Parliament's amending power is itself limited by the Constitution and cannot be used to convert that limited power into an unlimited one, and that judicial review is itself part of the basic structure and cannot be excluded even by constitutional amendment. Reinforces why courts retain review power over the very hierarchy and structure that Parliament might otherwise try to rearrange." },
    { name:"Supreme Court Advocates-on-Record Association v. Union of India (NJAC case)", cite:"(2016) 5 SCC 1; W.P.(C) No. 13 of 2015, decided 16 Oct 2015",
      facts:"The 99th Constitutional Amendment Act, 2014 and the National Judicial Appointments Commission Act, 2014 sought to replace the judiciary-led 'collegium' system for appointing Supreme Court and High Court judges with a commission that included executive representation. This was challenged as compromising judicial independence, which had been recognised as part of the basic structure since the 'Second Judges Case' (1993).",
      holding:"A 5-judge Constitution Bench, by a 4:1 majority, struck down both the 99th Amendment and the NJAC Act as unconstitutional, holding that the proposed composition gave the executive a role that compromised the primacy and independence of the judiciary in appointments — restoring the pre-existing collegium system. Shows how the composition and independence of the court hierarchy itself is treated as untouchable basic structure, not just the powers those courts exercise." }
  ],
  "juris:u2:1": [
    { name:"Kesavananda Bharati v. State of Kerala", cite:"AIR 1973 SC 1461",
      facts:"The petitioner, head of a religious mutt in Kerala, challenged land reform legislation restricting the mutt's ability to manage its properties. Heard by a 13-judge bench, the case became the vehicle for a larger question: whether Parliament's power to amend the Constitution under Article 368 was unlimited, following the earlier Golaknath ruling that fundamental rights could not be amended at all.",
      holding:"The Court held Parliament has wide power to amend the Constitution, including fundamental rights, but this power cannot alter the Constitution's 'basic structure' (rule of law, judicial review, federalism, separation of powers). Used to show the limits of pure command-theory positivism — a sovereign's command is itself subject to a higher constraining principle, which Austin's model struggles to explain." },
    { name:"A.K. Gopalan v. State of Madras", cite:"AIR 1950 SC 27",
      facts:"Gopalan, detained under the Preventive Detention Act, 1950, challenged his detention as violating personal liberty under Article 21, arguing 'procedure established by law' should require a fair, just and reasonable procedure, similar to 'due process' under the US Constitution.",
      holding:"The Court took a narrow, literal (positivist) view — 'procedure established by law' meant any procedure enacted by a validly passed statute, regardless of fairness. A textbook illustration of analytical positivism: validity traced to the correctly-enacted rule, not its moral content. Later overturned in Maneka Gandhi v. Union of India (1978), often cited as the shift from strict positivism toward natural-law reasoning in Indian constitutional interpretation." },
    { name:"Naz Foundation v. Govt. of NCT of Delhi", cite:"160 (2009) DLT 277",
      facts:"An NGO challenged Section 377 IPC, which criminalised 'carnal intercourse against the order of nature', arguing it violated dignity, privacy and equality. The provision had existed since the colonial-era IPC and was formally valid purely as a 'correctly enacted' rule.",
      holding:"The Delhi High Court read down Section 377 to decriminalise consensual adult same-sex conduct, relying on constitutional morality rather than mere pedigree of the rule. Shows Hart's 'rule of recognition' tested against a formally valid but substantively contested rule, and sociological/natural-law reasoning displacing pure positivist validity (culminating in Navtej Singh Johar, 2018)." }
  ]
};
